Civil rights groups issue travel warnings against Missouri
Due to discriminatory policies and racist attacks in Missouri, the American Association for the Advancement of People (NAACP) issued travel warnings to people of color who were preparing to travel to Missouri.
“The NAACP Missouri Travel Advisory, which took effect on August 28, 2017, calls upon all African-American travelers, tourists, and Missourians to pay special attention and take an extremely cautious approach when traveling in Missouri due to a series of suspected racial incidents that have recently occurred in Missouri,” the group said in a statement.
NAACP pointed out that the recent adoption of a Missouri law makes it more difficult for people to win discrimination lawsuits, and the state's law enforcement also targets minorities to some extent, which prompted the organization to issue travel warnings.
Violation of civil rights is happening to people.
They were interrogated, beaten or killed because of their skin color,” Missouri NAACP President Rod Champeer told The Kansas City Star.
“We have received many complaints, an unprecedented number.”
It was the group’s first such warning in the U.S. against a state.
The group cites racial slurs against black students at the University of Missouri and the death of Tolly Sanders, a 28-year-old black Tennessee male.
Earlier this year, Saunders died under suspicious circumstances. He ran out of fuel while traveling in Missouri and was detained by Missouri police without an alleged crime.
According to a recent report from the Missouri Attorney General’s Office, “black drivers in the state are 75 percent more likely to be stopped than whites.”
“This consultation is intended to raise people’s awareness and warn their families, friends and colleagues about what might happen in Missouri,” Chaber said.
“People need to be prepared, whether it’s carrying bail to Missouri or letting relatives know they’re traveling within the state.”
According to the latest data from the FBI’s hate crime reporting program, Missouri recorded 100 hate crimes in 2015; the state ranked 16th in the nation, based on the number of crimes.
Travel warnings are also in response to Missouri’s new law, which would make it more difficult to prosecute businesses for housing or employment discrimination.
Previously, U.S. states had passed immigration enforcement laws requiring local law enforcement to detain immigration violators, and the American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) said the move would increase the number of racial lawsuits and issued travel advice for Texas and Arizona.
Travel warnings are usually issued by the U.S. government against other countries, but more recently, advisory groups have adopted this approach in response to certain laws and trends within the United States.
