The BBC said that Beijing has become one of the most exciting cities: making foreigners feel "living in a new era"
Reference News Network reported on January 18 that Beijing may have a long history of three thousand years, but it is also rapidly transforming into today's modern charming city.
This Chinese capital has an amazingly high-speed Internet, cutting-edge technologies such as facial recognition software, invested heavily in artificial intelligence and unrivaled international energy, which is one of the most exciting cities for exploratory foreigners.
According to the BBC's website on January 16th, the German expatriate Clemens Szech said: "If I can succeed there, I can succeed anywhere in the future." He quoted singer Frank Sinatra singing the lyrics of New York City.
“If Sinatra lives to this day, he’s likely to sing a city like Beijing,” said Cech, who works as a creative director for Traveler’s Archives magazine. “It’s very likely that living in a city like Beijing will make you feel like you’re ‘living in a new era’ and always keeping up with trends.”
Simon Norton, who lives in England but has recently lived in Beijing, wrote in an article on his personal blog, 4Corners7Seas, saying: "There's generally a feeling of being at the center of some kind of big things, and it's hard to say, but it's got many foreigners to stay there. They want to see what it's going to be, and they want to see for yourself."
The city’s high-quality, high-speed internet and technology-friendly environment make everyday life easier than ever.
Andy Peña Fuerte, from Manila, who currently serves as deputy editor-in-chief of the Beijing Children website, said: “Beijing is rapidly transforming into a cashless society, and the Internet has integrated the functionality of traditional wallets into smartphones, making it easier for everyone to make many transactions.”
However, smartphones are only the beginning of Beijing's technological ambitions, facial recognition software has become very popular, and many mobile phone applications use this technology to make bank transfers and payments, get people into apartments and offices, and even use it to verify the identity of ride-hailing drivers.
Beijing also recently announced plans to spend 13.8 billion yuan to build an artificial intelligence technology park, where more than 400 companies will be settled, and they will focus on the development of various artificial intelligence technologies ranging from biometrics to self-driving cars.
Food is also the core of many major festivals in China over the years, participating in these celebrations is respected by foreigners, the Spring Festival is the most important social and economic festival, and it is also one of the most likely festivals for foreigners to participate.
“I propose to help Chinese friends prepare for the New Year’s Day, and it’s just as important to clean up their apartments and paste red springs with New Year’s greetings at home. On New Year’s Eve, it’s important for all family members to gather together for a big meal until midnight and greet the New Year with brilliant fireworks. It’s indeed an opportunity to integrate with friends and family.”
The number of foreigners in Beijing is large and growing, but China’s language barriers and the ease of communication between expatriates can easily serve as an excuse for their incomplete integration into local culture.
"Some foreigners have been living in China for many years, but there's almost no experience for their host country. If your initial attempts to reach out to the Chinese are not successful, it's easy for you to still get along with other foreigners."
In these so-called “foreign bubbles” (“foreigner bubbles” refer to foreigners mixing only among foreigners, without contact with the locals and local cultures), foreigners hold groups in their own communities, eat in Western restaurants, and have only foreigners among their friends.
The easiest way to overcome this is to learn a little Mandarin – it’s easier than one might think.
“One or two hours a week of Chinese private lessons or an entry-level radio drama should be suitable for everyone’s busy schedule.”
Even learning a few classic phrases can be helpful for shopping, ordering at a restaurant, or chatting in a taxi.
Recalcitrant air pollution is also a fact of everyday life, but in recent years the government has worked hard to contain some problems.
Residents stressed that it is important to go outdoors in warmer months that tend to be less polluting, and to use those blue sky days as an opportunity to wander around in historic hutongs or to explore the popular altar parks.
“Beijing is the Chinese city that can find the most things below the surface, and you’ll find great artistic and musical activities, emotional nightlife, historical wonders, ultra-modern business districts, and ancient hutongs,” Norton said.
