A picture to understand: Bitcoin's legal status around the world
In addition to the price correction naturally triggered by speculative attributes, the sharp decline of this round of digital currency is also related to the "old enemy" - regulatory risks.
Judging from the visual map, the attitude of Eastern countries towards Bitcoin is more conservative than that of the West, and the countries that account for the majority of the world are still waiting for the legitimacy of the digital currency, which means that the "black swan risk" is still very large.
*This article comes from Wall Street News (WeChat ID:wallstreetcn), editor Wang Mu. For more information, please visit wallstreetcn.com, or download the Wall Street News APP.*
As the regulatory storm in major bitcoin markets such as China, South Korea and the European Universies began, the global digital currency led by Bitcoin has been bloodbathed this week, and the price of Bitcoin has fallen below the $10,000 psychological integer bit on Tuesday, the largest one-day decline in three years.
In addition to the price correction naturally caused by speculative properties, the sharp decline of this round of digital currency is also related to the long-standing "enemy" - regulatory risks.
At this important juncture, the visual big data website HowMuch.net has created a map showing Bitcoin’s legitimacy and supervision in countries around the world.
As shown in the following figure, green represents the legal market of Bitcoin; orange represents the neutral market, although it has not yet publicly legalized Bitcoin status, but there are no major use restrictions; light pink represents the restricted market, that is, the use of digital currency by the local local government trying to pass the red line policy or supervision; deep pink represents the country where Bitcoin is defined as illegal; gray is the country that has not yet commented.
(Photo by HowMuch.Net visualization team on January 15)
The article pointed out that it can be seen from the distribution of non-green color blocks in the figure that the attitude of Eastern countries to Bitcoin in the traditional sense is more conservative than that of the West.
Russia is currently the largest country to define bitcoin as illegal, China is one of the largest restricted markets for Bitcoin, and India is one of the largest countries that has not yet commented on Bitcoin and has not yet introduced relevant policies.
North America and Western Europe are the regions with the highest levels of acceptance of Bitcoin, i.e. the more concentrated distribution of green blocks.
Countries in the Middle East and Latin America have significantly divided their views on Bitcoin, and the distribution of color blocks in these areas is more mixed.
Among them, Iraq, Iran, Turkey, Brazil are all legitimate bitcoin markets, Afghanistan, Pakistan, Saudi Arabia, Egypt, Bolivia, etc. There are some restrictions on its trading and use, and more Latin American countries have not yet spoken about Bitcoin.
Combining the above data shows that among the 246 countries subject to global statistics, a total of 99 countries (or 40%) do not impose restrictions on the transaction and use of Bitcoin, that is, green and orange areas.
There are 7 countries (or 3%) that are restricted markets (light pink), 10 countries (or 4%) define Bitcoin as illegal (dark pink), and 130 countries (or 53%) do not have more information about digital currency (grey).
This shows that countries accounting for global entities (53%, more than half) are still waiting for the legitimacy of digital currencies led by Bitcoin in China, which also represents that Bitcoin’s “black swan risk” is still very large, because some countries may eventually make legislative decisions to restrict digital currencies.
Regulators around the world have not yet fully understood the emerging category of digital currencies, which may be the main reason why most countries have not yet made a statement.
Although Bitcoin’s “ popularity” has grown exponentially since last year and does not mean that all countries are interested, some countries may be concerned that local fiat currencies will be threatened as a result, or that digital currencies will be exploited by criminal organizations.
Wall Street has written a detailed summary of the international regulatory news against Bitcoin since last week, including China’s efforts to crack down on online platforms and mobile apps for centralized services on over-the-art exchanges, South Korea’s ongoing consideration to shut down digital currency exchanges, the EU’s belief that bitcoin has not yet been widely accepted and warning investors of the risk of “losing money.”
The editor-in-chief of Wall Street has mentioned that from the perspective of supervision, acceptance/criticism and adoption, 2018 will become the first year of digital currency for global central banks.
Russia and other major countries to criticize the legitimacy of Bitcoin, in fact, is also to give way to the official issuance of digital currency.
