Now Trump's White House Is Targeting the Statue of Liberty
One of the most certain signs of regime change is when they started attacking statues.
Americans, like anyone else, agreed with this. As a result, Saddam Hussein of Paradise Square in Baghdad in 2003 was orchestratedly overthrown.
As one of the Trump administration’s key think tanks, Stephen Miller is engaged in a heated debate with CNN’s Jim Acosta over the president’s proposal to severely restrict legal immigration, he is sure to know what’s going to happen when he slashes a symbolic axe at the Statue of Liberty.
To be fair, Miller didn’t attack the statue itself.
If he did so, then a group of cruise boat owners and liberal followers would have devoured him alive.
But he did attack the significance of the statue, especially in 1903, when the famous poetry of Ema Lazarus was engraved on the pedestal (the monument was built 17 years later).
As Miller rebuked Akosta for saying, “I don’t want to talk about all the facts of history here, but the Statue of Liberty is the symbol of America’s freedom that illuminates the world.”
The poem you're referring to, that's added later, not part of the original Statue of Liberty."
In fact, Miller was right, but as he himself said, it wasn’t really about history.
The only country that seems to invite not only any old immigrants, but also the poorest of the poor: “Your exhaustion, your poverty, or your squeezing people are eager to breathe freely, and those who suffer reject your crowded beaches.”
There’s no “suffering man who refuses” on our screen to become more dazzling at the moment.
Miller hasn’t worked on literary commentary, and he’s made it clear that these people won’t be popular in Trump-era America.
This kind of conflict about the significance of statues is part of a broader political and cultural war: it actually conflicts with the American spirit.
Lazarus, along with her friends who held an event after her death engraving her verses on the pedestal, was well aware that the behavior they were involved in was highly political.
The purpose of the sculpture is to express the connection between French and American republicanism through ancient classical metaphors that serve as the femininal incarnations of the Statue of Liberty.
Lazarus changed that: in her poetry, the female image is no longer abstract, she has to speak.
She gave herself a very different name: the mother of exile.
The combination of this poem with this painting was a successful feminist coup d’état and was a devastating blow to American nativism.
Just as Lazarus changed the meaning of the statue, right-wingers have always wanted to change it back.
Rush Limbaugh, a baptist of Trump’s arrival, argued in 2010 that liberalism was not inviting anyone, and that he was now an early neoconservatist who brought the flame of American wisdom to the world of ignorance: “The goddess of freedom is bold.”
She meant to bring the torch of freedom from the United States to other parts of the world.
The torch is not to illuminate America’s path.”
Unsurprisingly, Limbo was actually moved by something.
The significance of the statue depends entirely on the perspective of cognition.
The most imaginative practice of Lazarus was to be able to see it. When weary, poor, but hopeful people would see it on the deck of the ship after a long and often terrible journey.
On the surface, this involves the view of the world from the perspective of those who seek asylum and have the opportunity to improve their lives.
Her statue conveys to them the original intention of the hope and welcome that they need to hear.
Of course, that’s not what Miller and Trump once wanted to admit.
Their torch is not intended to illuminate the way, but to ignite the hatred of their only source of strength.
