2017 World Championships: Neil Black praises the Scottish players in the British national team
Eilidh Doyle recalled that there were only two Scotons in the British national team at the time.
Neil Blake, executive director of the British Athletics Association, said the team of 16 strong Scotsmen in the world championship group would make a "significant contribution to the national team".
The number of Scottish field athletes selected for the 2017 London Athletics World Championships, which opened on Friday, is the highest in history.
“Scotland is making extraordinary developments” and British athletes must learn from it.
“We accepted and managed to understand that, and we’re going to persevere until we’re successful,” Black said.
"I'm going to talk to people from the Scottish Sports Academy. They think it's about what they're doing.
Scottish track and field athletes believe this has something to do with what they did.
The British Athletics Association believes that this has something to do with their actions.
Even people on the streets think so.
No matter what happens, it’s brilliant.”
A number of Scottish athletes are medalists, including Laura Muir and Andrew Butchart , who will compete on 5,000 m with Mo Farah, while Eilidh Doyle has been chosen as captain by the group.
“Ered is a bit modest.
“It’s unbelievable her captain’s speech,” Blake said.
“Many athletes, whether they’re Scottish or not, no matter where they’re living or training, are proud to be out of the door.
The passion and true feelings that Erid is naturally invested in are very good.”
The Commonwealth silver medalist, who will compete in the 400-meter hurdles in his fifth World Championships, said it was “incredible” that there were 16 Scots on the British national team.
Previously, the number of Scottish athletes participating in the World Series was up to seven, which is the number of participants in 1983 and 2015.
Muir is the leader. In the past year, she has set five British records and two European records.
She will compete in the 1500m and 5000m events.
Chris O’Hare, who competed in the 1500-meter event, is another Scottish player to watch for, having previously achieved good results during the season, winning in the British national qualifiers and the annual Olympics.
Doyle recalled that she played in the first world championship, when the only other Scot on the team was Lee McConnell.
The captain said that she is very proud that so many of her compatriots not only joined the team, but in some cases will also challenge medals to reach the finals.
"What's special about being captain of the national team is that it's voted for by the rest of the national team. So knowing that your team-mates voted for something extremely unusual, it's a great honour for me anyway," she added.
“Of course, I’m very proud of all the Scottish players.
It just shows how far we’ve come.
And they are all the old track and field athletes here, and they are about to move forward with the purpose of winning the podium.
How was my captain’s speech?
Yeah, I think it's great.
I have just referred to the fact that in this case we are very lucky because we held the world championships at home.
That’s why it’s really important that we’re able to grow with home support and because we’re able to get back to the Olympic Stadium.
I’m going to get a second chance to play, and it’s going to be awesome.”
