Block critics on Facebook: If you can't stand the censure of voters, don't run for office
Our Founding Fathers understand the style of bad media.
The Americans loved Washington, but soon after, the major newspapers began lashing out at him for everything, from domestic policy to political principles.
He irritated these criticisms, no doubt.
However, he did not silence these critics.
Because as early as 1783, Washington said, “Freedom of speech is taken away—we will be slaughtered like lambs in silence and ignorance.”
It reminds me of Maryland’s Gov. Larry Hogan, who needed to learn from Washington.
Hogan’s employees blocked and deleted posts from at least 450 people because they posted their views on his official Facebook page.
The American Civil Liberties Union filed a lawsuit against him earlier this week.
The governor’s staff described the lawsuit as hasty, and online reporting was rife with their backlash: “Who cares?”
Many people say, “It’s just Facebook.”
However, this is important.
This is especially important, because it’s against people like Hogan.
It’s a Republican who serves as governor in a state with a democratic majority, and he’s surprisingly popular.
Among the country's 50 governors, he was ranked second.
Hogan is not a reactionary impatience.
He was in charge of Maryland, strictly following the rules.
He is also very good at using Facebook as the main way to connect with voters, jokingly showing his baldness after chemotherapy for the first time on his homepage.
So it's completely unnecessary and ultimately very dangerous to block people coming to the governor's homepage. The home page is a public forum, an official management platform that is advertised by public tax administrators.
In an interview with The Washington Post, Hogan’s spokeswoman Amelia Shas defended the governor’s behavior, saying she thought blocking their comments was nothing more than trying to make them restrain.
But they unswervingly describe it as a frendom or cynics sweeping the network.
The Washington Post spoke face-to-face with people who had been shielded by Hogan.
They’re just ordinary people talking to their chosen leaders: they’re teachers, small business owners and pastors, and they’re not a monster.
They have said that their comments have depth, respect, and are not indescribable.
Citing the Bible in his post, the pastor called on Hogan to remember his Catholic faith.
Bowie's barrister in Bowie, Maryland, was not cynical, but she asked questions about hate crimes.
She wrote in the Washington Post’s news story commentary section, “I’ve commented politely that I’m disappointed that he didn’t respond to hate crimes and other recent news events.
I also don’t like a bunch of people commenting on the same topic, because they’re directly ignored by some people who think they’re the so-called herd mentality, rather than treating them as a bunch of worried citizens.”
What the governor’s clerks called a “densive spam bombing” may seem to others to be “persistent.”
The Facebook era has made it easy to change a piece of information by simply blocking a critic or deleting a negative comment.
It’s much cleaner than it used to be, and to do the same effect in the past, it’s going to send employees out to collect and burn newspapers with criticism or to arrest or silence protesters.
But that’s what’s going on right now, it’s just at the numerical level.
Hogan isn’t the first public official to be blamed for silencing others on Facebook and other social media.
President Trump is being sued by Twitter users because he blocked them on Twitter.
Last week’s landmark ruling emerged in Virginia on such issues.
According to District Judge James C. Kacharis of Alexandria, the chairman of the Lawden County Oversight Committee’s blocking of voters from her Facebook page violated the First Amendment.
Kentucky Governor Matt Bevin (R) has also been patronized by the American Civil Liberties Union for his use of Facebook and Twitter.
It shouldn't be so difficult.
In the Washington era, affordable postage and the Internet had a similar impact.
The number of newspapers quadrupled between 1776 and 1800, and others wrote anonymous letters criticizing his leadership fiercely.
And even at that time, Washington also had an anonymous frenzy against him.
Pseudonyms such as Yurikla, Valerius, Belisarius, and Pogius were used to write letters to newspapers critical of Washington’s decision-making.
According to historical documents collected by George Washington’s online project, his office was swamped with petitions criticizing his attitude on the Treaty of Friendship, Commerce and Navigation between His Majesty the King of England and the United States of America, signed with Britain.
But he didn’t silence them.
Freedom of speech, freedom of dissent, and freedom of speech are the foundations of this country.
Real leaders need to accept this.
