Ronan Lafferty is looking forward to enjoying the feeling of coming home at the Renaissance Club
The East Lothian Renaissance Club hosts the 25th Scottish Evergreen Open, where former European champion Ronan Laverty will enjoy feeling home for the next three days.
Last year, the Northern Irishman led Archerfield Links next door to the final, but eventually lost to Paul Ires. He now works for the new venue, representing the club's founder and CEO, Jerry Salwati, as an event ambassador.
“I’ve witnessed the founding of the Renaissance Club, and we’re going to witness its glory through this competition.
Athletes will see the best of the stadium and the best.
Jerry created this place incomparibably.
He’s a keen champion of the competition and he’s also very proud of his accomplishments here.
It's the best stage to show this stadium."
The Englishman Ires is looking forward to defending for the first time, although in his 32-year career he has won at the same time the European Tour and the European Challenge Tour.
The Southport athlete said: “It’s really special.
“I didn’t defend the Extremadura Open because in the second year I won, the game was cancelled.
It’s amazing to come to this place.
It’s great to play golf here.
Last year's memories surfaced, and for me and my wife Sharon, last year's win had a special meaning for us.
It's really a special memory, and we'll always remember it."
Returning to his home country was Gary Orr, who turned 50 earlier this year and then took part in the evergreen tour of Europe.
This is the first time he has competed in Scotland since the 2013 Scottish Golf Open.
Helensburg Oll said: "There will always be more meaning to go back home to the game.
You want to do well, but you can feel the extra pressure again.
I really enjoyed being able to play again, I’ve been playing golf.
I'm happy with the current situation."
Joining the trio are former Masters winners Ian Woussnan and evergreen champion Paul Broadhurst, Roger Chapman, Mark James and Mark McNewti.
There were six Scottish athletes on the field, two of whom were Oor and former Ryder Cup captain Sam Torrance, and the other four were Andrew Audecoren, Stephen McAllister, Bill Langmue and Ross Drummond.
