Poverty and Death in Jinzhou, Indonesia
Budtina DeJ didn’t need a stove when she was cooking.
She doesn’t even use pots.
Her wooden house is located in Enarotali, the capital of Paniai County in the Indonesian province of Papua, and the housewife usually just puts a sweet potato – locally called “nota” – directly into the fire.
After half an hour, this charred sweet potato was taken out by a pair of hands that had not been washed, and devoured.
Dai Jie was sitting on the mud floor—she had no furniture, and it was also a place where she made everyday family chores, such as washing her clothes, which were very muddy, all from nearby swamps.
There is a bucket in a room without a roof, and this is the outdoor toilet.
She was the youngest of her husband’s four wives, so she wasn’t taken care of by the fields.
Polygamy is common here.
It’s very dangerous, of course, it’s dangerous to work until later: most of the men in the village don’t have a job, and a lot of them’re drunk, plus there’s a lot of soldiers here.
She said, "No one dares to walk around the village after 5 p.m."
This is a rare glimpse of everyday life in the Papua highlands, once a colony of the Netherlands, which was annexed to Indonesia after a referendum in 1969, when only 1,026 elders were forced to vote in front of the army occupying it.
The ongoing push for independence and opposition to Dutch rule will soon point the finger at the Jakarta government, which imposes repressive rule in the region and isolates foreign journalists and activists.
In 2003, the province was officially divided into Papua and West Papua provinces, while Papua New Guinea independently took control of the eastern part of the island.
Enarotali is remote and deserted; it takes 90 minutes from the provincial capital, Jayapura, to Nabire, and then rent a car for 5 hours, all the way to the mountains.
There is no public transportation here.
In this town of about 19,000 people, the wooden houses are surrounded by bamboo fences, and the folded iron roofs are all kinds of yellow-brown coverings made of rust.
Very few Indonesians have come, not to mention journalists and almost no foreigners.
Prior to the arrival of the Christian evangelists, Mee Pago Bábua believed in a god named Uga Tamee.
There are other changes here too.
“We don’t get used to these clothes,” Dai said, referring to her brightly coloured hand-weaving headscarves, dark shirts, and bright skirts.
“We used to cover our bodies only with leaves.”
Papua is Indonesia’s poorest province, with 28 percent of the population living below the poverty line and the region with the highest infant mortality rate and the lowest literacy rate in Asia.
But it’s also Indonesia’s Golden Island.
Paniai County is located on a high tract, about the same area as New Jersey, with 153,000 people. The world's largest and most profitable gold mine, Grassberg, is only 60 miles away, owned by Phoenix-based Felipe McMoran Copper Gold Company.
In 2015 alone, Felipe mined about $3.1 billion worth of gold and copper mines here.
In addition, Papua’s timber resources are abundant, with an estimated value of $78 billion.
However, these rich resources are also the source of the Papuan tragedy, and Indonesia's strong military strength has been able to suppress them.
According to a 2005 New York Times investigative report, Felipe paid nearly $20 million to local military personnel and troops between 1998 and 2004, up to $150,000 for an official alone.
Papuans' demands for independence became more and more strong, which threatened the gold, and they were subjected to cruel treatment.
According to activists, more than 500,000 Papuans have been killed since 1969, and thousands have been raped, tortured and imprisoned by Indonesian forces.
According to the Asian Human Rights Commission, the mass killings in the Babua tribal highlands in the 1970s had escalated into genocide.
Last year alone, Indonesian police arrested more than 3,900 peaceful protesters.
A 2016 report from the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Brisbane, “We’ll Lose Everything,” included proofs of what had preceded the previous year, such as extrajudicial executions, torture – and another report that rape and electricity were particularly prevalent – as well as a brutal crackdown on peaceful demonstrations.
Andrias Halsono, an Indonesian researcher at Human Rights Watch, stated that “violence happens on a daily basis, so it is difficult to count the number of victims”.
Papua's resources attracted a large number of immigrants outside Indonesia, and the oppression began.
Eighty-three percent of the province's population of 3.5 million are Christians, but the demographic structure of Muslim economic migrants has changed after coming here from the densely populated islands of Java, Kalimantan, Sumatra and Sulawesi.
The Javanese food stalls sell fried chicken and vegetables mixed with peanut butter.
The locals are struggling.
The 24-year-old Abos-You, a 24-year-old, has moved to the provincial capital, Chayapura, to look for a job, explaining that “those immigrants started selling chicken and vegetables at lower prices in traditional markets than local Papuans.”
This has caused the local Papuans — the mama-mama – to lose their markets.”
Indonesian President Joko Widodo (often referred to as Jokowi) vowed on his 2014 election campaign to address Papua’s inequality and abuse of power.
The former carpenter’s path to Jakarta’s presidential palace was supported by 27 of all 29 districts — including Pania.
But nothing has changed in Papua, and today the locals feel betrayed.
“We’re heartbroken, and in 2014 we voted for Jokovy and hope he can live up to our aspirations and restore fairness.”
In fact, just two months after Jokovi took office in October, Paniai had its worst nightmare.
On December 7, 2014, 11 children in Enarotali were singing Christmas carols in front of a bonfire outside, and two Indonesian soldiers suddenly rushed over on a motorbike.
The frightened children told them that they should turn on the headlights.
According to the local pastor, Yavit Tebai, one of the soldiers was dissatisfied with their tone, and then returned with four soldiers.
The victims and witnesses said the drunken soldiers used their guns to chase the children.
Then one of the soldiers shot at the children.
The 16-year-old, Eulianus Yemer, was severely beaten in a coma.
A few hours later, a raging fire broke out at the Election Committee building of a nearby government agency, and the incident escalated the next day.
About 1,000 young Papuan men, women and children gathered at a football stadium in front of local police stations and military command centres to demand access to justice.
With ceremonial bow hunting, they danced the waita dance of the Mee Pago tribe of Papua – running in circles and imitating birdsong.
Some of the protesters began throwing stones at frequent military posts.
According to an official document seen by Time magazine, when emotions continued to intensify, the soldiers received orders through internal radio: "If the crowd resists more than three times, shoot immediately." But the document was not released to the local media.
Jeremiyas Kayamu, 56-year-old leader of the Kego Koto neighbourhood of Enarotali, sensed that danger was about to occur, he appealed to the crowd to calm down and implored them to go home.
But no one was in the mood to listen to him.
Standing on the porch of his brightly coloured wooden house, he told Time magazine: "When I turned back, my left wrist was suddenly shot."
Kayamu still doesn’t know who fired the gun, but he said the bullets came from among the assembled soldiers.
He said, "It's very crowded, and they're firing a lot of guns."
According to the cousins of local Olfius Yule, he witnessed the shooting and saw Orfius Yule being shot three times.
Yuhanes sadly told Time magazine, “I ran over to check his body to see if it wasn’t him.
I found out that he was dead...I kissed him."
Papua Police Chief Inspector Juje Mende told reporters that his subordinates were simply “protecting” the police station because it had been attacked.
In 2015, Papua police spokesman and police chief Pugio Sulistio said: “When someone threatens to kill us, we have to defend ourselves.”
It’s a matter of death.”
According to Human Rights Watch, five young protesters were killed and many more were injured.
News about the killing spread the next day only slowly in Jakarta.
Three weeks later, Jokowi gave a speech in Jayapura expressing deep sympathy to the families of the victims and vowing to address the abuse of authority left over from Papua’s history.
“I hope that this case can be resolved as soon as possible, so that it will not happen again in the future,” he said.
In October 2016, Security Minister Wilando said he wanted to set up a non-judicial mechanism to address the historical legacy of human rights violations.
But excuses are also almost immediate.
He said, “Most of the violations took place long ago.
Some were in the 90s and early 2000s.
The point is that we are already committed to addressing these violations, but that still requires a process.”
Then on June 5 in Jakarta, Verando, in a dialogue with Time magazine, said he had no plans to establish a grievance mechanism in Papua.
On the contrary, he said, “everything will be settled in accordance with the law”.
The United Nations has filed a complaint against Wilando for more than 1,000 deaths in a bloody independence vote in East Timor in 1999, but said 11 human rights violations in the Papua region had been resolved, including the Paniai incident.
The families of the victims of the Paniai incident scoffed at these statements.
“I’ve been interviewed four times in the last three years, but things haven’t progressed at all,” Yuhanes said.
Years later, he said, "I'm tired."
He is still living in fear.
He said, “I’m scared.
I was afraid of being arrested by the army and afraid of being shot.”
His brother Yukabos also believed that the people of Panier were afraid to talk about the incident.
He said he was beaten by the military after helping to bury the four victims.
He said, “After the body was buried, the military came to me.”
The shooting didn’t stop.
On Tuesday, Indonesian police shot villagers in Deyai County, Paniai neighboring prefecture.
A construction company manager refused to help transport a comatose man to the hospital, clashing, killing one person and wounding 17 others, including children.
A 24-year-old man, Lavianus Doughou, drowned while fishing in a nearby river and died on his way to the hospital.
Angry villagers protested in front of the company’s on-site office.
Police said villagers threw stones at the police, so the police fired warning shots.
However, riot mobile forces (Indonesian paramilitaries) began to shoot at the crowd, and one person was killed.
Doming Valley Bardy, 29, told Time magazine, "We were all panicked at the time, and now we're afraid they're going to take revenge." He lived near the hospital when he witnessed the wounded being rushed to the hospital.
“I’ve been hiding at home for two days.”
Decay's parliament has called for the police officers involved to be brought to justice and the riot mobile forces evacuated from the area.
Paniai has always been a wrong place for the Indonesian government.
Deficiencies in effective development grew the grievances of Mee, Moni, Dani, and Damal tribes, which were scattered in the lush central highlands of Pania.
Many have joined the Free Papua Movement (OPM), a rebel army that claims to protect the rights of the Papuans by dispersing raids and raids to abduct Indonesian soldiers.
Some of the top leaders of the Free Papuan Movement were born in Paniai, including Tadius Yugi and Daniel Yudas Cogoya.
As a result, in recent years, thousands of Paniais have been arrested by the military under the guise of “maintaining national sovereignty” and deliberately detained.
Some have never appeared again.
In the eyes of the Papuans, Paniai is a "forgotten place of tragedy."
Poverty breeds discontent.
Enarotali sells poorly little rice, and the locals simply can’t afford it.
Bread is out of reach at all.
People here grow everything they can eat: mainly sweet potatoes and some fruits and leaf vegetables.
Cultivation is a woman’s job, and every woman is able to take care of four or five sweet potatoes.
They usually use most of the harvest to retain writers, and the rest is sold in the local market.
Ten sweet potatoes are only worth 10,000 Indonesian rupees (75 cents).
Over time, economic inequality has widened among local Papuans and newcomers, and the number of these migrants has increased significantly since the opening of a new route to Nabire Airport.
A small number of existing jobs often fall into the hands of better-educated and wealthier immigrants.
Papuans have very little money or the necessary skills to run competitive companies.
John Gobai, President of the Paniai Tribal Council, said: “Young people don’t want to stay in this village ... because there’s no work and no money.”
Papua is isolated from the world.
In addition, the restrictions on international media reports are still tight.
Earlier this year, French journalists Frank Escudy and Basel Lunchang were expelled from Papua because they “don’t cooperate with relevant agencies,” even though they have been granted permission to shoot.
According to Human Rights Watch Asia’s deputy director, Felim Kaine, Jokowi’s vote to lift reporting restrictions to promote transparency and development has not materialized.
In a statement, he said: “If foreign journalists try to cover the disgruntled provinces of Papua and West Papua in the easternmost part of Indonesia, they are at new risk: denying visas and pulling them to the blacklist.”
The absence of media scrutiny means that international pressure on the Indonesian government is largely confined to Papua’s neighbors.
In March, six Pacific nations — Tonga, Nauru, Palau, Tuvalu, the Marshall Islands, and the Solomon Islands — asked the UN Human Rights Council to investigate “all kinds of widespread human rights violations” in Papua, including the Paniai shootings.
These countries have also supported the Free Papua Movement .
Indonesian Foreign Ministry spokesman Almannata Nassir, who is indifferent to the group’s allegations, told reporters in Jakarta that “Indonesia is still implementing democratic institutions and media freedom, so the evidence of human rights violations is difficult to conceal.”
Local people want more attention from foreign governments.
According to a person familiar with the matter, on May 4, when Dutch human rights ambassador Keys Wan Barr led a delegation of officials to Chayapura, the local people broke their silence and begged for "we want to be free." He also attended the meeting, but requested anonymity.
Indonesia will hold its next presidential election in 2019, but Papuans say they are unlikely to vote in Jokowi again.
Gobai, chairman of the tribal council, stated that "Zokowi's intentions are good, but he is surrounded by people who were involved in the Paniai shootings."
He wanted Jokovy to know that the people of Paniai were not only living under the threat of a greedy army, but also had nothing, and that there was very little education in the region, and that public health care services were scarce.
Gobai said the people of Paniai, like other Papuans, believe that the vote they cast for Jokovi was the debt he had to repay.
“They don’t need money, they need justice.”
Even under threats and intimidation, the families of Paniai’s victims carried out their last symbolic revolt: burying the bodies of one of the victims across from the military police post.
They know that justice may never come, but at least they won’t let those culprits forget their crimes.
Yukabos said with his head down, “My family has been killed.
“What else can we do?”
With the help of capital forces such as Sophia, Hao Laike, Dinggu and other brands are using capital strength to customize the home industry, and on February 10, 2017, the China Securities Regulatory Commission approved the initial application of Guangdong Shangpin Home Distribution Home Co., Ltd., and issued a small and medium board listing pass on the Shenzhen Stock Exchange.
