The fire once again engulfed Dubai's "torch" skyscraper.
On Friday, a fire broke out at one of the world’s tallest residential buildings in Dubai, forcing hundreds of residents to flee the 337-meter-tall, 79-story supertall building.
It’s the second blaze in just two years to sweep through a high-rise building that happened to take place in what’s known as the “torch.”
The building has been evacuated, there have been no reports of injuries, and the cause of the fire has not yet been found.
“We’re sleeping and then we’re awakened by the fire and people started screaming.
It takes about 10 minutes for us to run down the stairs and from the 50th floor,” a resident named George told Reuters.
Civil protection authorities in Dubai said the fire brigade had extinguished the blaze around 4 a.m. local time and was cooling down the building.
The Government indicated that efforts were being made to provide shelters for those affected.
This accident may once again raise concerns around the world about the safety of high-rise buildings using external wall materials.
An investigation conducted by the management of the " Torch" skyscraper in the aftermath of its 2015 fire found that most of the objects damaged were cladding exterior panels for decoration and insulation.
British police have said they believe problems with the insulation and cladding system at London’s Grenfell building may have contributed to the rapid spread of fire in June, which killed about 80 people.
The Williams team also hinted that Massa was likely to return, although he had already announced his retirement.
On January 9, only one month away from the opening of the Pyeongchang Winter Olympics in South Korea, with the pre-heating of the Olympic flame from the south to the north and various preparations, the Winter Olympic atmosphere in South Korea has become more and more intense.
South Korean public opinion is looking forward to this long-lost inter-Korean talks, which can bring an "egg" to the countdown to the Pyeongchang Winter Olympics for one month.
On November 1 last year, Pyeongchang Winter Olympic flame arrived in South Korea.
The boom set off by the flame is directly reflected in the ticket sales of the Pyeongchang Winter Olympics.
Just more than two months ago, Pyeongchang Winter Olympics ticket sales rate only hovered at 32%, the Winter Paralympics ticket sales rate was only 4.3%, the sluggish ticket sales rate once made the outside world worried about the popularity of Pyeongchang Winter Olympics.
However, as Li Xifan, chairman of Pyeongchang's Winter Olympic Organizing Committee, predicted to reporters not long ago, "With the transmission of the Winter Olympic flame in various parts of South Korea, the heat of the Winter Olympics is increasing, and it is believed that ticket sales will soon improve."
According to South Korean media reports, as of January 3, Pingchang Winter Olympics ticket sales have reached 64%, and the ticket sales rate of the Winter Paralympic Games has also exceeded 50%.
In order to encourage foreigners to go to South Korea to watch the competition, the Ministry of Justice of South Korea also announced on the 8th that it will extend their stay in South Korea for foreigners who visit the Pyeongchang Winter Olympics, with a maximum stay of 120 days.
The survey found that more than one-fifth of U.S. travelers are carrying prohibited items on board.
An investigation in response to aircraft passengers found that more than 20 per cent of passengers had knowingly or unintentionally clipped illegal items through a U.S. Transportation Security Administration checkpoint, and at least 6 percent of those passengers had unintentionally carrying prohibited knives or other bledges on board.
Less than 1 percent of passengers said they had only since been found to be traveling with guns, ammunition or explosives.
The survey of more than 1,000 people was conducted by charter service Stratos Jet Charters Inc., which also found that younger travelers are more likely to blatantly ignore regulations.
Among the respondents who admitted that they had tried to deliberately board flights with TSA prohibited items, 19.7 per cent were millennials, compared to less than 15 per cent in Generation X populations.
The most common items that deliberately slip through TSA security personnel are food and beverages.
More than 3 per cent admitted that they had deliberately carried a bladder through security, while 2.2% of female respondents and 3.7 per cent of men said they had knowingly brought prohibited drugs on board.
According to the company, travelers across the country were surveyed to find out the number of people who accidentally carried and deliberately carried embargoed goods through the security check-in of the plane.
Unsurprisingly, it is those most likely to carry embargoed goods through security—intentionally or unintentionally—those who fly the most frequently.
However, the company points out that the TSA guidelines are a bit complicated and difficult to understand.
People are often not sure what and how many liquids they can carry.
So it’s not surprising that TSA seizes the most banned liquid.
At one point, it was thought that TSA might also use books as an embargo.
Liquids are allowed on domestic flights, but international flights are not allowed.
U.S. Environmental Protection Agency Administrator Pruitt changes his mind over Obama-era emissions-reduction regulations
A day after being sued by 15 states, Environmental Protection Secretary Scott Pruitt changed his earlier decision to delay the implementation of the Obama-era rules to reduce emissions from smog air pollutants.
Pruitt offered to make the change, because the Environmental Protection Agency was able to respond more quickly to the needs of state agencies for environmental regulation than previous administrations.
He did not mention the legal challenge brought by the Federal Court of Appeal against his previous position.
Controversy has focused on setting October 1 as a deadline for states to begin meeting ground-level ozone standards in 2015.
In June, Pratt announced that he would delay compliance for another year to give the Environmental Protection Agency more time to study the plan and avoid “interfering with local decisions or hindering economic growth.”
Prior to his appointment by President Donald Trump, Pruitt was Oklahoma’s attorney general, which has long opposed the cyclicization of environmental regulations.
Since arriving in Washington, Pruitt has repeatedly turned to regulations that prevent or delay opposition in the chemical and fossil fuel industries.
Wednesday’s abrupt reversal was an up-to-date legal obstacle to Pruitt’s return to the agenda.
Last month, a U.S. Court of Appeals in Washington ruled that the head of the Environmental Protection Bureau overstepped his authority to try to delay enforcement of the Obama administration’s rules requiring oil and gas companies to monitor and reduce methane leaks.
In a statement released on Wednesday night, Pruitt offered a U-turn in his approach to ozone standards, saying he wanted to strengthen the Environmental Protection Agency’s commitment to work with states to adopt complex procedures to meet new standards.
“Under previous administrations, the EPA usually doesn’t meet a specified deadline, and then waits for radical groups and others to sue and then agrees to agree to a designated timetable through an agreement,” Pruitt said. He has filed more than a dozen indictments against the Environmental Protection Agency in his previous work.
“We don’t believe in the regulation going through litigation, and we’re taking deadlines seriously.”
We are also serious about regulations and authorities.”
Nonetheless, the EPA’s statement said Prout might again use his “delay authorization and all other legally available authorizations” to ensure that the provisions are in line with “good policies and the best available information.”
Republicans in Congress are pushing for a broad rewrite of ozone-layer regulations.
The House bill approved last month is intended to delay the implementation of the 2015 rule by at least eight years.
This measure has not yet been voted on in the Senate.
New York Attorney General Eric Schneiderman led a coalition of states this week to sue the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency, saying it intends to maintain legal pressure.
“The U.S. EPA’s inversion — after we filed the lawsuit — is a significant victory for the health and safety of 6.7 million New Yorkers, with more than 115 million U.S. communities directly affected by smog emissions,” Schneiderman said.
California, Connecticut, Delaware, Illinois, Iowa, Maine, Massachusetts, Minnesota, New Mexico, Oregon, Pennsylvania, Rhode Island, Vermont, and Washington, and the District of Columbia all joined New York to participate in the case.
Ground-based ozone is formed by the reaction of common pollutants emitted by automobiles, power plants, refineries, chemical plants, and other sources under atmospheric sun exposure.
The resulting smog can cause severe respiratory problems to sensitive populations, causing thousands of premature deaths each year.
Of course, public health advocates and environmentalists cheered Pruitt’s surprising shift.
Lori Ann Byrd of the Centre for Biodiversity said: “It is disturbing that this common-sense measure has come under intense pressure from the heads of the department responsible for protecting the air we need to breathe.”
“We have to fight the Trump administration’s ideological movement to cater to polluters and groups of special interests.”
