Since the second half of 2009, Xiaoshan flower and wood sales (value) has maintained a high trend, especially this spring, Xiaoshan seedling sales hit a record high, sales increased sharply, prices rose sharply, according to statistics, from January to June this year, Xiaoshan District to achieve seedling sales revenue increased by 48% compared with the same period last year, the growth rate is the highest in nearly 20 years.
Shi Haiyong, deputy director of the Xiaoshan District Forestry Bureau, said that with the good sales situation, Xiaoshan flower and wood production has also achieved rapid development.
In the past two years, the planting area of Xiaoshan flowers has expanded by 30,000 mu, and the total area has reached 180,000 mu; at the same time, it has actively developed outside the area, and Xiaoshan Miao farmers have established flower and wood bases throughout the country to reach 80,000 mu, an increase of 30,000 mu over 2008.
While the planting area is expanding, the quality of seedlings has also been continuously improved, the value of nurseries has been continuously improved, large specifications and high-quality seedlings have been retained more, and some seedlings have been sold.
Shi Haiyong also reminded that in the face of the current boom in production and sales, producers should calmly face, objective analysis, not only to see the society's demand for flowers will be long-term and increasing, but also to realize that the market competition will be more intense.
On the morning of July 13, Beijing time, Somaltus filed a lawsuit in the Eastern Texas District Court, accusing Apple of infringing on a battery technology patent it applied for in 2010.
The company has previously sued Asus, Lenovo, Samsung, Sony and Toshiba with the same patents.
According to the lawsuit, both the iPhone 6s and similar Apple devices have infringed U.S. patent number 7,657,386, which is known as the Integrated Battery Services System.
Plaintiffs wish to receive cash compensation, or charge a patent fee for the infringing equipment after the judgment was announced.
Judging from the indictment, the plaintiffs appeared to believe that the iPhone’s fast-charging feature infringed on the company’s patents.
iPhones with fast charging will quickly charge up to 80%, and then trickle-down charging.
Somaltus is actually a so-called “patent rogue” because the company doesn’t offer obvious products and services, and it’s difficult to find relevant information online.
However, they have successfully reached an out-of-court settlement with automakers such as Ford and Nissan for the patent.
