Moor Beach tragedy: activists re-enact racist lynchings, justice
A 1948 Ford Mercury car runs through a group of onlookers in the small town of Munro, Georgia, rumbling towards the tiny Moor Beach Bridge.
There were four black passengers and a white driver in the car, and before the car arrived at the bridge, a group of white people with guns walked forward, blocking the road of the car.
The thug’s head — a middle-aged man in a fine-striped suit — took a deep sip of his cigar and glanced into the windshield.
Passengers in the car were full of fear.
“We want – Roger!” cried the man.
“Take him out of the car!”
A young negro was dragged down from his front passenger seat and pierced the silence with screams.
Fortunately, this terrible scene on July 22 is just a re-enactment.
However, when "Roger" put a noose around his neck, almost all the onlookers made a real respite.
Since 2005, civil rights activists have returned to the Moor Beach Bridge, recreating the scene of two black couples – Roger and Dorothy Malcan, as well as George and Mere Dossy – who were hanged by the K-K party that night in 1946.
No one has ever been charged with murder.
“While we know who they are, even though we’ve seen them in the community, it’s incredible that none of them have been arrested over the years,” said Tyron Brooks, who helped organize this re-enactment.
“This is the stain of history, the burden of our souls.”
Brooks, 71, is a former Georgia lawmaker and lifelong rights activist.
For him, re-enactment is an eye-catching call to action, reminding people in the Monroe community every year that injustice has never been corrected.
"We want to prosecute, we're going to close the case, we're going to heal, we want reconciliation, but we have to get justice first," he said.
“Unless we receive justice, we will not be healed or reconciled.”
On July 27, 1946, the coroner covered the body of a lynching victim in Moor Beach.
According to FBI files obtained by NBC News, more than 50 men from Monroe and surrounding counties are suspected of involvement in lynchings.
However, none of them have ever been prosecuted.
While the suspects have been with impunity for decades, civil rights activists say the dual motive for lynchings is well known throughout town: politics and sex.
In April 1946, a Supreme Court ruling allowed black citizens in Georgia to vote in primaries for the first time later that summer.
According to FBI statistics, around the same time, black tyrannon Roger Malcolm stabbed white landowner Barnett Hurst in a struggle — it looked like it’s for a woman.
Brooks said there were rumours in town that Hirst had slept with Malcolm’s wife, Dorothy, and that her baby wasn’t her husband’s either.
According to the FBI, on July 25, 1966, prominent white landowner Roy Harrison spent $600 to release Malcolm from prison.
Dorothy and his cousins, M. Murray and George Dorsey, accompanied him to the prison.
However, as they came back from the prison and were about to reach the Moor Beach Bridge, a group of lynching thugs surrounded the car.
“It turned out that it was Harrison who organized and ordered the execution of this murder,” an FBI report concluded.
“It is clear that there is a conspiracy between state and local law enforcement who were not only involved in the murder, destroying the evidence, but also concealing the identity of the witnesses.”
Brooks is very concise.
He said, “This is a voting rights tragedy.”
"They were brutally killed, and the murderer sent a message to black people in this community: 'If you register and vote, you're going to have the same ending.'"
For nearly a decade, Cassandra Green has been in charge of an annual re-enactment that dominated the lynching of the Moor Beach bridge.
Her manipulative image is vivid and gripping, with a deeply racist background. Many people hope that this racism no longer exists in the United States.
It’s hard, it doesn’t look harder, but that’s the key to what Green says is.
“We don’t want to talk about lynching because lynching makes us have an ugly face for everyone,” she said.
"But if you don't listen to me, or if you don't hear me talk about my experience, I can't have a relationship with you."
This year’s show began in June, with nearly 30 actors — more than a dozen whites, the rest black — huddled in a room on the first day, listening to an overview of the rehearsed shows Green released.
Wade Malbow serves as co-director of Green.
Over the years, he has been playing the role of the Klux K party, but he said it’s never easy.
“I felt dirty because I wasn’t that kind of people,” Malbow said.
“But I think it’s very important to keep this history alive, because we don’t want to go back to that era.”
In the room, actors playing Roger Malcolm and Barnett Hirst, Darios Bradshaw and Nick Raskona, were rehearsing their lines.
At the beginning of the show, Malcolm yelled and pushed away Hirst, angry that he had slept with his wife, Dorothy.
“Let’s get out of my place!”
Rascona shot back.
And then they fought.
Then everyone (at least all the black actors) burst into laughter.
Green laughed the most.
“What’s the matter?!” asked Rascona.
He was panicked and confused.
Have I played too much?
“No,” Green said with a laugh.
“I just don’t think the white man won’t say ‘ah’ when he hits back.”
“Yes,” another black actor quipped.
"Your tone must be tough."
In this way, the energy in the room has changed; the feeling of fraternity drives away the discomfort.
Everyone strives to do this, not only to perform the play, but also to commemorate the four victims.
The scene begins again.
This time, when Rascona said the word "ah", the tone was sharp and powerful.
No one laughed.
The repetition came to an end powerfully and quickly.
Screams, gunshots, and then there was silence.
A woman dressed in funeral black stood by the actor’s body and sang Billy Halede’s words of mourning “Strange fruit.”
Her voice pressed against other words.
One hundred yards away, about forty spectators were constantly wiping sweat and tears.
Blacks and whites accounted for almost half of the audience.
There is a couple who come to see the show from Ithaca, New York, and there are many people who live near the road.
Brooks took them to the cemeteries of Malcolm and Dorothy, nearly eight hours away, and everyone was suffering from Georgia's uncomfortable feeling of humid heat.
Until this time, the murderer was still a fascination, but now, standing in the same land where the lynching took place, there was anxiety in the air.
When the song’s final note disappears, Green asks people to step closer to inspecting the actor’s body.
The children leaned down, looked at false blood and corpses, whispering to each other.
“They’ren’t really dead,” a little boy muttered as if to remind himself.
A few minutes later, Greene thanked the crowd, and then the show was over.
The actor throws away the lynching rope and wipes away tears.
Bradshaw embraces Malbow, a suit man who plays the head of the Three K parties.
Nick Rasconer hugged two of his colleagues and weeped in their arms; the emotion in the show eventually infected him.
“It doesn’t matter,” one of the women whispered.
“We know that’s not the case in your heart.
There's a reason you're doing this."
After that, the audience sat down to have dinner with the actors.
At the same table, a white couple from Atlanta chatted intimately with a black couple they had just met, talking about re-enacting things.
Denis Duplinsky had a hard time trying to find words to describe how she felt after watching.
“It’s hard to imagine that those horrible words and actions . .
“It’s true for me to think the lynching is still going on,” she added, “and now it’s only called police shootings.”
In the room, Tyron Brooks sat at the table watching, tasting and reminiscing about civil rights.
He said he’s already started planning for next year’s
“Reenactment is an inspiring way because it describes the horrors we’ve experienced, but it’s also very educational,” he said.
“If you don’t learn from it, it could happen again.
Those who don’t study and don’t know their history are destined to repeat them.”
