U.S. media: Chinese Air Force Engineering University scientists study laser satellites for the removal of space junk
U.S. media reported on January 18 that Chinese scientists have put forward a bold and innovative proposal to use large lasers to clean up cluttered Earth orbit and destroy old satellites and their sky garbage.
According to the U.S. "Newsweek" website reported on January 15th, researchers at the Chinese Air Force Engineering University wrote a paper entitled "Space-based laser removal small-scale space debris de-oralization model study", which describes how space debris can be irradiated into smaller, less harmful fragments by using space-based platform lasers.
Space junk has been a concern of the International Space Agency for years, and NASA has considered a variety of options for tracking, detecting and removing man-made debris.
Means of removing large-scale space debris include a giant network for cleaning and collecting waste, as well as magnets that can be used to suck them out of orbit. Chinese scientists have conducted laser simulation studies and concluded that this will be an effective way to clean up the Earth's orbit.
According to the abstract of the paper, “(simulation studies) provide the necessary theoretical basis for the application of space debris removal technology on space-based platforms.”
A 2014 study by U.S. defense giant Lockheed Martin found that there are about 200 threats per day for satellites that are orbiting.
High-speed debris moving along Earth's orbit is seen as a serious threat to future space missions, described by European Space Agency experts as a "deadly cascade of threats." High-speed debris has continued to increase over the past year.
According to the U.S. "Popular Mechanics" monthly website reported on January 17th, space is basically empty, but next to Earth's space is becoming more and more crowded. Each satellite we send into space will eventually become another piece of debris orbiting the Earth, and over time, these fragments will pile up.
The more debris there are, the higher the chance that a satellite that is still operating will be hit by debris, which in turn will damage or destroy a multimillion-dollar investment.
According to the report, all space agencies have plans to remove space junk in the future.
NASA is considering reducing the speed of the debris with the jet gas, de-orbiting it, and Europe is considering launching a satellite carrying a large network to capture the debris and bring it back to Earth, and the idea is for Japan to capture the debris with an electric chain.
At present, China has also proposed a solution to space junk, that is, using a large laser to hit floating space junk into less harmful small fragments.
According to a group of researchers from the Chinese Air Force Engineering University published a paper published in the German journal Optics, installing such a laser on a satellite will be enough to clean up the space efficiently, at least according to the simulation results.
According to the report, the only way to confirm the validity is to test, there is no arrangement for such a mission, but it may be sooner or later that someone will install a laser on a satellite and send it into space.
Once that’s done, we’ll finally be able to start eliminating the problem of space junk once and for all.
(Compilation / Hu Jing, Wang Haike)
