Pearson lays off another 3,000 jobs, slashing dividends to revive business
British education group Pearson said on Friday it would cut another 3,000 jobs and slash an interim dividend of 72 per cent as it embarked on another restructuring to deal with business pressures.
CEO John Farron said that most of the job cuts will take place in the second half of 2018 and the first half of 2019, after the previous restructuring has reduced 4,000 jobs.
Pearson announced plans in May to cut another £300m ($394 million) in costs in its third cost-cutting program to revive businesses hit by the rapid development of digital learning.
Last month, the company agreed to sell its stake in Penguin Random House to partner Bertelsman and published a report saying its basic sales rose 1 percent in the first half of the year to £2.05 billion, but said the outlook remained unchanged this year.
