Suicide bombing of convoy in Kabul kills NATO soldiers
According to official sources, a military convoy was hit by a suicide bomber in the Afghan capital, Kabul, killing one NATO soldier and injuring several others.
NATO said five soldiers and an interpreter were injured.
According to unconfirmed local reports, three civilians were also killed in this incident in the city’s Karabagh area.
It's the latest violent attack in Afghanistan -- Tuesday's Herat mosque bombing that killed more than 30 people.
On Monday, a suicide bomber killed two people near Iraq's embassy in Kabul.
According to a NATO statement, an explosion occurred late on Thursday, and joint patrols of the Afghan and NATO coalition forces were attacked with "individually carrying improvised explosive devices".
The statement said that all the injured are currently in stable condition and are being treated at a U.S. military hospital at Baghram Air Base.
According to reports, the growing presence of the Taliban and the so-called Islamic State (IS) group in the Karabakh region has raised serious concerns among NATO forces stationed near Baghlam.
According to the United Nations, at least 1,662 civilians were killed in Afghanistan in the first half of 2017, while deaths in the capital accounted for about 20%.
At least 30 people were killed by a suicide car bomb attack in the predominantly Shiite area of Kabul at the end of July.
The Taliban claimed that they carried out the attack.
On May 31, a huge explosion in central Kabul killed more than 150 people, the deadliest militant attack in the country after a U.S.-led multinational force toppled the Taliban in 2001.
The violence highlights the dangerous security situation in the country as U.S. President Donald Trump weighs on whether to increase Afghanistan's support for its army and police.
According to reports, the suicide bombing in Kabul in recent weeks has also triggered a crisis of public trust in the government and its policies, particularly President Ashraf Ghani’s reputation.
The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) overturned the resolution and delayed smoke regulations after litigation.
During his tenure, Mr. Obama called on states to curb all types of emissions that caused smog, and 15 states sued the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency because of it. A day after the indictment, the EPA overturned it.
The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency on Wednesday announced its decision to pursue the so-called “Ozone for 2015” and promised to work with states.
U.S. EPA Administrator Scott Pruitt said in a statement, “We are confident in engaging in dialogue with national partners and are committed to responding to them.”
Pruitt announced in June that the EPA plans to postpone the implementation of the ozone-compliant area standard until October 2018, with a one-year delay – according to which existing smog pollution is measured in some areas to determine which areas should reduce emissions from smog pollution to comply with air quality standards.
Fifteen major Democratic states, as well as the District of Columbia, filed a lawsuit on Tuesday, claiming it was illegal.
As part of the broader agenda, President Donald Trump’s authorities have struggled to revive the various environmental regulations pushed by former President Barack Obama to spur economic growth.
But these efforts have sparked opposition from Democrats and conservative groups. They fear that more emissions will affect public health.
According to the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency's website, breathing ozone-containing air "will reduce lung function and increase respiratory symptoms, such as asthma."
