McKee Garcia and Vasily Lomachenko meet for the first time to explore possible future battles.
The most high-profile 147-pound boxing match will take place soon, with Vasily Lomachenko and McKee Garcia meeting for the first time on Thursday.
In their separate rooms in the ESPN office in Los Angeles, the undefeated World Boxing Council lightweight champion Garcia took the lead in walking down the hallway to pay tribute to Lomachenko, the world's boxing organization's super featherweight champion.
At the Showtime boxing on Saturday, the umpire unanimously determined that Garcia had defeated the four-division world champion Adrian Brona to victory, so far, both boxers had been among the top five players on the same heavyweight list.
Garcia stretched out his right hand and said, “Champion, how are you?”
Good luck to you."
During an ESPN game at 7 p.m. on Saturday, Lomachenko defeated Miguel Mariaga, preserving his status.
When the two of them took the picture, Lomachenko’s agent, Egis Klimas, pointed out that the potential showdown between the two players depended on the “perfect size”.
“I used to be lightweight,” Garcia said.
“I’m going up against Brona (140 pounds), but I’m not that big.
Compared to 140 pounds, my weight is still a little worse.
Let’s go back to lightweight.
And he has to get to the lightweight (135 lbs) before we can fight.
That’s what the fans want too.
Then we will satisfy them.”
Lomachenko agreed with that.
"The boxing world needs this battle."
Klimas asked him if he should pay, and part of his strategy would be to get Lomachenko to make his debut match with a 135-pound player later this year, thinking he would make a date with Garcia (37-0,30 knockouts) next summer. Garcia is also interested in a showdown with Jorge Linares-Luke Campbell's victor after the Sept. 23 Forum Lightweight Championship.
"Because all the fans are asking this question, this battle may be pay-as-you-go," Garcia said. After that, he was open to playing games on networks that offered the best financial options.
“We’re the main people.
It’s impossible for others to have so much attention.
As long as they’re ready.”
Garcia praised Lomachenko as "an excellent player" and was likely to watch his boxing match on Saturday.
But Lomachenko hesitated about Garcia's praise, saying he thought the three-division champion from Riverside looked "slow. Sorry, McKee ... you're going to get knocked out."
Garcia said the focus is to show the strength of his fist, and then boxing.
They all said they could feel the momentum of their boxing matches.
“It’s a battle that needs to happen,
Attention and enthusiasm are beyond the reach of other boxing matches.
“I’m happy to play,” Garcia said.
Lomachenko said: “I’ve been waiting for the top fighters and this time it’s McKee’s turn.
It's going to be a great fight."
