China's National Development and Reform Commission officials' statements on the early completion of the 11th Five-Five emission reduction targets have given the steel industry with a strong expectation for the resumption of production.
Although at present, steel mills have not received a clear notification from the local government that the energy conservation and emission reduction work has been completed, the industry estimates that around December 15, 2010, steel mills that were originally limited to production in various places will gradually complete the resumption of production, and steel prices may begin to come under pressure.
At present, it has not yet been notified of the end of power restrictions, but in fact, the pressure of various enterprises has been much smaller than that in September and October, a private steel company in Hebei Province told reporters.
Some places in Hebei have repeatedly issued power restrictions and limited production documents, and at one point, they have taken the force to restrict the production of enterprises.
The reporter learned from local enterprises that at present, steel mills in most areas of Hebei have returned to work, and the pressure to limit production has eased.
My steel network analyst Zhang Tieshan expects that because the north has entered winter, the terminal demand for construction steel will gradually go from strong to weak.
Coupled with the gradual end of energy conservation and emission reduction, by December 15, most steel mills will basically resume production, and the products will arrive in various markets before New Year's Day.
The increase in the supply of resources will put some pressure on steel prices, which are currently rising.
