The latest results of the age assessment test for asylum seekers in Sweden
The Swedish asylum test uses the latest method to medically assess their age, and the latest results show that more than 80% of the subjects were judged to be more than 18 years old, but not everyone believes in its accuracy.
Sweden’s National Center for Forensic Medicine (R?ttsmedicinalverket) began testing earlier this year.
Accused of an accurate assessment of the age of some of the refugees claiming to be minors, the Swedish Migration Agency (Migrationsverket) designed these tests with a view to conducting a more accurate age assessment during the asylum process.
To date, 6,880 tests have been sent by the Swedish Migration Agency, and the Forensic Medicine Center has released the results of a total of 2,481 test takers between mid-March and July 31, 2017.
Eighty per 80 per cent (2002 persons) were judged to be 18 years of age or older, but in 25 other tests, the Swedish National Forensic Science Centre determined that the subject “may be 18 years of age or older”.
In 432 tests, the result was that the person “might be under the age of 18.”
The test is only for people who have questions about their age.
According to John Granson of the Center for Forensic Medicine, these results are consistent with those of the previous months.
He told The Local: “There’s not that much difference between the data of the previous few months, and the numbers are very consistent.”
The higher percentage of cases judged to have reached the age of 18 does not necessarily mean that these asylum-seekers lied about their age.
In May, the center released 581 test results, saying 442 “indicated that the subject was 18 years or older.”
Svenska Dagbladet, a news paper, investigated 581 test results and found that 243 of them had publicly stated that they would turn 18 this year.
X-ray examination of wisdom teeth, MRI scans of joints, and then analysis by dentists and radiologists, this method of medical evaluation has also been called into question in some areas.
One of the skeptics was Claude Marcus, an endocrinologist at Caroline College, whose work included assessing people’s biological maturity to determine whether they experienced puberty earlier or later than their normal age.
He told the newspaper Metro: “There’s a reason to think so, and I think the measurement used shouldn’t be like that.
It is very inaccurate to determine biological age in this way.
If we can't guarantee accuracy, we shouldn't pretend we can do it."
However, Gramson of the Forensic Medicine Center defends the accuracy of these tests: “It can be said that there is a very small risk of uncertainty in determining that a child is an adult.”
The biggest risk is that the person is just under the age of 18, which is about 10%.
West Virginia Governor Announces Change of Party
On Thursday night, West Virginia Democratic Governor Jim Justice announced at President Trump’s rally that he would change the party.
“If you like it or don’t like it, the Democrats are gone from me,” the governor’s judge said.
“Today, after a lot of prayers and a lot of thinking, I’m going to tell you ... I’m going to tell you today as a West Virginian, that I can’t help you as a Democratic governor anymore.”
There were cheers at the rally.
“Tomorrow, I’m going to change my membership to the Republican Party,” Jastis added.
"As a coach, I'm going to tell it's time to start another game."
Trump had earlier promised a major announcement at a rally in West Virginia.
Mr. Trump won 42% of the vote in West Virginia, and Jastis did not support Democratic candidate Hillary Clinton.
According to Forbes, Jastis was voted the state’s only billionaire in 2016.
According to Forbes, he inherited his father’s coal industry and owned coal mines in all five states.
At present, his worth is about $1.59 billion.
At the same time, the Republican National Committee issued a statement on Thursday evening saying: "Governor's change of party is yet another blow to the Democratic Party, which will not prevent our country from becoming great again, but will make our country great again."
“There are currently only 15 governors in the Democratic Party, and controlling the number of state legislatures at an all-time low, Governor Jastis’s statement is the latest rejection of a party that has lost leadership from top to bottom and can’t find a positive unified slogan,” Republican National Committee Chairwoman Rona McDaniel said in a statement to the CBS news.
