When I write songs, there is only emotions, there is no story line, and it has always been a pity.
Sebastian Coe praises Russia's 'candid' apology for doping scandal
On Thursday, the president of the International Association of Athletics Federation, Sebastián Coe, praised Russia's apology for the doping scandal, saying the apology was "candid", but he reiterated that now is not the time for Russia to return to the sport.
Mitri Chiracdin, president of the Russian Athletics Federation, told an IAAF convention on the eve of the World Athletics Championships that it was right that his country was banned from participating in the sport and that he was determined to fight doping.
He said he regretted that “all athletes who won gold and silver medals in the competition were stripped of their medals”.
Despite his comments, the IAAF voted in favor of maintaining the ban, following a report by the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA), which found evidence of Russian state-sponsored doping, which was implemented in November 2015.
"I think today's response was very candid and very candid," IAAF President IAAF Ke told reporters.
“The entire Council and the IAAF are pleased ... The Russian Federation acknowledges that it has gone through a period of very burning and is doing everything possible to ensure that Russia is able to return to the competition.”
Ke said he was also “happy” that Russia had accepted the criteria for recommissioning.
“I think it’s been a very constructive day and I think we’ve made some progress, but the IAAF supports the task force’s recommendations, and it’s not the time to reinstate eligibility for Russia,” he said.
"We need to do everything we can in the next few months to normalize the situation."
“Our guiding principle has always been the desire to separate innocent athletes from contaminated systems,” he added.
Nineteen Russian athletes, meeting the strict standards of the IAAF, will compete as neutral players at the World Athletics Championships in London on Friday.
The head of the IAAF task force, Wune Anderson, said on Monday that Russia had yet to meet several criteria for resuming competition.
After speaking to the IAAF council in London, he told reporters that drug testing is still insufficient, and banned coaches are still coaching.
