What is the impact of football’s financial rules on Neymar’s move to Paris?
Rules designed to prevent unrestricted spending by luxury European football clubs will not prevent Neymar from moving from Barcelona in Spain to France's Saint-Germain (PSG) at record prices.
As a result, the European Football Association (UEFA) has further relegated Saint-Germain in Paris. The European Football Association is the governing body of European football and has the right to prohibit teams from participating in the much-watched Champions League.
UEFA’s introduction of the Financial Equitah Rules (FFP) in 2011 means that Paris Saint-Germain Football Club will eventually have to prove that Neymar’s transfer received financial support without causing huge losses.
FC Barcelona has paid 222 million euros ($262 million) just to pay for the Brazilian player’s release, while Neymar’s salary will be as high as tens of millions of dollars a year.
Here are some of the financial implications of Neymar’s move to France.
Considering that the team’s funding comes from the financially coached Qatari ruling family, Paris Saint-Germain Football Club can certainly sign Neymar with cash and include him in the ranks of other stars.
Clubs can spend heavily to buy players, but they have to take advantage of legitimate sources of income to make up for the huge amount of money they spend, so that they can break even in football-related businesses.
The Financial Fair Play Rules were conceived by Michel Platini after he was elected president of UEFA in 2007.
The former French player believes that for those clubs that are highly indebted in pursuit of success, if the bank or everyone withdraws their support funds, they are actually defrauding and risking the club’s destruction.
In 2011, the rules shifted from club debt issues to the club’s loss monitoring.
This change means that the business man that Roman Abramovich used when he acquired the Chelsea club in 2003 is to buy a club with potential, and then spend a lot of money to improve the team and build a global brand, and is now faced with a more demanding situation.
During the three-year assessment of the Rules as of 2018, the club is likely to lose €30 million.
Although Abu was disparageded by his then-president in 2015, UEFA’s successor, Aleksander Ceferin, remains committed to halting excessive spending.
“Of course the Rules haven’t failed yet, and we’re definitely going to step up regulations in the future,” Andrea Traverso told The Associated Press last week.
Although Paris Saint-Germain Football Club will pay Neymar's buy-out terms at one time, it can also be distributed within the duration of the contract according to accounting purposes.
Neymar has signed a five-year agreement, so the transfer fee is likely to be shown at €44.4 million in its annual account for the next five years.
The first wave of “rules” sanctions imposed in 2014 imposed the harshest sanctions on Paris Saint-Germain Football Club, as well as the Manchester City club in Abu Dhabi.
Paris Saint-Germain was fined 60 million euros ($82 million at the exchange rate at the time), later reduced to 20 million euros, and asked the team to be reduced to 21 players in the 2014-15 Champions League, rather than the normal 25 players.
UEFA is staring at clubs that are trying to increase their income by pegging to club owners, deceiving financial regulators.
While Qatar’s company can sponsor Paris Saint-Germain Football Club, UEFA won’t see it as a legitimate income if the sponsorship amount is considered to exceed standard market prices.
That’s what happened in 2014, when UEFA ruled at the time that the Qatar Tourist Board’s sponsorship of Paris Saint Germain was overstated.
According to reports, Neymar's income from Paris Saint-Germain Football Club may come from payments made by the organizing committee of the 2022 World Cup in Qatar.
However, Paris Saint-Germain Football Club and the organizing committee both denied that Neymar would serve as an ambassador for the World Cup, and it would be easy for UEFA's regulators to make such a move.
The recent sanctions imposed on Saint-Germain in Paris means that UEFA will be more wary of any attempts to circumvent the rules.
People familiar with the matter said on Thursday that UEFA had not held prior discussions with Paris Saint-Germain on Neymar's transfer.
All of them contacted The Associated Press only on condition of anonymity because they were not authorized to discuss the status of Saint-Germain Football Club in Paris.
According to the last published accounts, Paris Saint-Germain had operating income of 542.4 million euros in 2015-16, with a profit of more than 10 million euros.
The accounts for the previous fiscal year have not yet been published.
Paris Saint-Germain Football Club expects Neymar's membership will bring a boon to sponsorship events, franchising and jersey sales.
The club expects revenue to grow by at least 30 percent after signing Neymar.
It’s clear that seeing one of the most famous players leave, the Spanish league is deeply outraged, trying to block Neymar’s move, and claiming that Paris Saint-Germain Football Club violated the Rules.
La Liga chairman Javier Tebas told the Spanish newspaper AS that "it's an obvious example of a 'financial stimulant', with clubs and countries alike," referring to Qatar, which has ultimate ownership of the Paris Saint-Germain football club.
Arsene Wenger, the head coach of the Arsenal team (whose owner of the club is an American sports tycoon), has reservations about the attitude of state-funded clubs and La Liga.
“It’s a consequence of ownership, and it’s revolutionized the entire ecology of football over the last 15 years,” Wenger said on Thursday.
“Once the country has a club, everything happens.
This will make it very difficult to comply with the Rules, because for a country there are many different ways or various interests to demand that big-name players represent a country.”
Rokid, a developer of intelligent interactive robots, has won a new investment led by Temasek.
In recent years, startups in the field of artificial intelligence have continued to be popular with capital.
Artificial intelligence Rokid recently completed a new round of financing, led by Temasek, Credit Suisse, CDIB (Taiwan China Development Financial Holding Company's investment institutions), the old shareholder IDG Capital and other institutions to invest.
It is reported that the scale of this round of financing is about 100 million US dollars, Credit Suisse and CDIB for the first time in the field of domestic artificial intelligence financing transactions.
Rokid is an intelligent interactive robot developer, committed to research in the field of robotics, products can control smart devices at home, with continuous independent learning and growth functions, but also with recognition, understanding, feedback and other functions.
This round of financing will mainly be used for research and development, talent introduction, strategic promotion and investment layout of the industrial chain.
Previously, Rokid had received $8.3 million from IDG, linear capital, Mfund, and Yuanjing Capital in August 2014. In October 2015, the company also received tens of millions of US dollars of A-round investment from Huadeng International and angel investors; on November 1, 2016, Rokid completed a B round of financing led by Shangji Capital, IDG, Mfund, Yuanjing Capital, and Huadeng International.
At the time, Rokid said the company was valued at $450 million.
In May 2017, Rokid released the consumer-grade intelligent voice robot “Raki Moonstone”, which combines artificial intelligence and home scenarios with a price of 1,399 yuan.
The robot uses speech synthesis, natural language understanding, speech recognition technology and microphone array technology, with chat conversations, playing music, and smart home functions.
Tech For A Better World and Home Audio/Video Components And Accessories (CES) and the German IF Design Award, known as the Academy of Design.
At just-concluding CES 2018, Rokid released a new product: consumer-grade Augmented AR glasses RokidGlass.
The glasses, which will be used as stand-alone headsets without the need to connect smartphones or external computers, are expected to be commercialized in 2018.
Previously, the company has released the first generation product home robot RokidAlien and the second-generation product smart speaker RokidPebble.
