The Bank of England will maintain an interest rate of 0.25%.
The Bank of England will announce that interest rates will remain unchanged at a later date, after weak economic growth and easy inflation have dashed hopes of an upward hike.
The Bank of England has warned that economic growth will continue to be “weak” as it tightens interest rates while tightening household incomes.
Policymakers on the bank’s Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) voted 6-2 to maintain an interest rate of 0.25 percent, while a handful of members this month called for higher interest rates, even as weak economic growth has dampened the boost.
In its quarterly inflation report, the Bank of England shrunk its growth forecasts for 2017 and 2018 to 1.7% and 1.6%, respectively, and warned that the tightening of household incomes will continue, while inflation will skyrocket to nearly 3% in the autumn.
However, it said interest rate hikes would be needed over the next few years to curb inflation caused by Brexit, while borrowing costs may need to rise more sharply as expected in financial markets.
Large-scale economic promotion policies were implemented a year ago due to the effects of Brexit, and members also voted to withdraw them.
The Bank of England will announce a halt to its regular financing scheme, which will provide cheap funding to banks starting next February, although it said it would now need an additional £15 billion based on the plan.
In the minutes of interest rate decision-making meetings, the Bank of England said: “In MPC’s core forecast, gross domestic product (GDP) remains weak in the near future, as tighter real household incomes will continue to dampen consumption.”
With regard to interest rates, the bank repeatedly reiterated the need for “certain monetary policy tightening” to suppress inflation, and it needs to be “greater” than market expectations.
At present, the market is expected to achieve its first growth in the third quarter of next year and the second in 2020.
However, the bank stressed that any increase will be "slow" and "restrained".
The bank’s growth forecasts for this year and next are on a downward trend, up from 1.9% and 1.7% in May.
The bank maintained its forecast of 1.8% growth in 2018.
The pound fell against the U.S. dollar, followed by the euro.
The pound fell 0.5% against the U.S. dollar to 1.31, while the euro fell 0.4 per cent to 1.11.
Prior to the decision to remain unchanged, rising upward expectations were dashed by the recent gloomy growth figures. GDP rose to 0.3% in the second quarter, compared with just 0.2% in the previous three months.
The bank said growth in the third quarter is likely to remain at 0.3%, although it will rise slightly at the end of the year.
Ordinary households have been hit by soaring inflation, while wage growth has been insignificant, and the Bank of England's recent inflation report did not give them good news, because the bank said that austerity will get worse before it gets better.
He added that monetary policy “can’t prevent” an impact on incomes over the next few years, but wages are expected to recover “massively” by the end of a three-year forecast.
At the same time, due to the depreciation of the British pound and the substantial increase in demand for British goods, the economy will be stimulated to offset some of the impact of low consumption.
A year before the decision was made, a shocking EU referendum sent the pound plummeted, with interest rates cut to 0.25 per cent in August.
The Bank of England estimates that it could take four years to fully impact prices and the British economy.
Ahead of the bank’s announcement of its latest interest rate ruling, there were calls for an increase in interest rates, as inflation added to the pressure on ordinary households, while three decision-makers called for interest rates to rise to 0.5% in July.
Inflation retreated to 2.6% last month from 2.9% in June, although the Bank of England said this was expected and will rise again in the next few months.
The bank said that compared to its target of 2%, the excess part has been the final bottom line of the weak sterling.
Andrew Montereyke, director of Coreco Mortgage Brokers, said: "Given the recent decline in swap rates, it seems that the mortgage market will once again become more competitive, as lenders strongly call for more business and look forward to a positive end to this year."
Such a market looks particularly advantageous for those who may be nearing the end of existing transactions, as remortgages (many of which are fee-free and have low interest rates) are becoming increasingly popular.
