UK’s National Health Service (NHS) has a green light for controversial HIV drugs.
After the High Court dust settles on the NHS’s drug financing, patients will be able to get “transformative” HIV preventive treatment starting in September.
NHS England has announced that pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP), a drug that can be taken before sex, has been shown to reduce the risk of infection in high-risk groups by about 86 per cent. Health services will now deliver the drug to an estimated 10,000 people through a three-year initial trial.
It agreed to provide funding for the trial after an appeals court upheld the High Court ruling last year. The ruling said the NHS was entitled to pay PrEP fees despite the NHS's claim that local authorities should fund the drug.
The move announced on Thursday has been described as a “significant new intervention” by British NHS CEO Simon Stevens, while the National AIDS Trust (NAT) described it as a “critical moment”.
Sexual health clinics in London, Brighton, Manchester, Liverpool and Sheffield will be among the first to be registered sexual health clinics. More clinics will be added in October, followed by full implementation in England in April 2018.
Pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) is an HIV drug taken before sex to reduce the risk of HIV infection.
Trials have shown that PrEP can significantly reduce HIV transmission in people at risk of infection.
Taking PrEP before sex means there are enough specific medications in the body to prevent HIV infection.
This medication can be taken daily or two hours before sexual intercourse.
Those who are eligible for treatment include gay, bisexual men, transgender people, and non-succeived HIV carriers.
The clinic will determine whether participants eligible and agreeing to the trial, including men, women, transgender people, and their sexual partners, who have received antiretroviral treatment for HIV status are unknown.
Those who live in England and are registered with a general practitioner can also sign up at the sexual health clinics they attend locally.
The scheme will cost £10 million, while the NHS England branch has signed a contract to procure these drugs this week following an internationally competitive procurement.
The trial aims to assess PrEP’s full potential by collecting clinical evidence about how it is positioned, absorbed, and implemented on a large sample basis.
“This significant new intervention should complement and strengthen HIV prevention broadly and increasingly successfully.
This is yet another milestone in more than 30 years of action worthy of progress in addressing the major human health challenges.”
Deborah King, chief executive of the British AIDS Trust (NAT), said: “This is a pivotal moment in the fight against AIDS.
If it does target people who are in need and at risk, pre-exposure prophylaxis offers the possibility of changing the AIDS epidemic in the UK.
Starting in September, people at high risk for HIV will be able to obtain, through the NHS-funded trial in England, a new tool that can be truly implemented alone, without the need to negotiate with sexual partners, which will bring improvement to the lives of many people.
We are in favour of this announcement with both hands.”
Terence Higgins Trust CEO Ian Green said: “The first priority now is to ensure that trials are rapidly rolled out across the country and that none of people living with HIV can fall behind.
Now that PrEP we’ve purchased trial drugs, we’re ready to protect more than 10,000 people at high risk of HIV.”
Sharon Hodgson, Shadow’s Minister of Public Health, said: “After months of delays and delays by the government, PrEP’s start-up work was very popular and should have arrived sooner.”
There is evidence of how this drug can change as part of our approach to preventing HIV and stopping the transmission of this deadly infectious disease.
This trial will allow us to go further and fully understand PrEP’s advantages.
It is now important to roll out this trial as soon as possible across the country in order to protect people exposed to HIV and to help us further end HIV transmission in society.”
