High quality of life: the application of loft and clever design to make the small apartment of Piaget Palace into a spacious home
Joanne Ray was a former banker who was passionate about renovating houses, and in 2014 she moved from a large house on Knight’s Bridge to a small apartment in Count’s Palace.
This apartment is located on the first floor of an elegant Queen Anne-style terrace - Joanne didn't pack her bags to sell it, which is a strong proof of the new design.
“Before that, my ex-boyfriend and I were living in a much larger house at Knightsbridge,” Joanne said.
“I was looking for the right house with an offer.
I saw this apartment at the count’s palace, and its ceiling was very high.
I made an offer the same day.”
The 1,000-square-foot apartment has now been completely overhauled since it first looked at the room.
The apartment used to be a well-lacused single room — which required walking through the kitchen to get to the bedroom — and Joanne wanted to add storage spaces and lofts to take full advantage of adequate ceiling heights.
“I wanted high ceilings, big windows, plenty of light.
“That’s what I’m asking for,” she said.
To do this, she hired Erfan Azadi from Duck & Shed's architecture and design studio (located in Notting Hill) to use this 12.5-foot-tall ceiling to carefully build a home. The home is not only a development project, but also where Joanne lives.
The height of the ceiling is not large enough to add two upper and lower spaces, so the architectural challenge is to create a loft that is high enough for people to stand.
Ervan has to be creative in order to solve this problem.
“The steel structure is quite complex,” he explained.
“We have to arrange the space so that it’s displayed up and down, and we don’t have to shrug up and down.”
It’s not just as simple to build a simple platform, it’s also about starting these steels so that they’re at the right height to implement their functions.”
Through this arrangement, Erfan was able to get two full-length rooms in the space – which adds many interesting elements.
The loft features retro copper windows under the Retrouvius brand and overlooks the charming living area.
Golden furniture with mirrors, small pieces of silver furniture and Serge Mouille’s retro lighting laid the overall style.
The use of mirrors plays an important role in adding atmosphere, space and light.
A mirror on both ends of a short corridor can make it look longer.
In the kitchen, a splash-proof board with a mirror is like a window leading to another room.
In the porch, small lattices fitted with mirrors provide Joanne’s storage space, which is used to store cutlery and glassware.
The beautiful living room has an attractive sense of space, and it is able to put away the kitchen when it is not in use.
The folding kitchen is not new.
But this folding kitchen is unique in that it is a garage-style lifting door.
“There’s knowledge in some areas of engineering here,” Elvan explained about the reinforced veneer MDF door.
“It’s a balancing principle, so it’s easy to slide when you push the door.”
Thanks to the skilful use of space and storage, Joanne got an additional 16 square meters, an extra bedroom, and an improved layout.
“Ultimately, you’re selling the size of the home — or your valuation is based on the size of the home,” Erfan said.
“Basically, real estate agents walk into the room and calculate it based on the size of the floor.”
They will try to increase the space, while using high-quality materials, thus bringing a sense of luxury to this space.
According to Elvan, “the material is your personal selection, so it helps to create an emotional bond in your home.”
“You don’t fall in love with the power on the wall.
It's all very important, but it won't buckle your strings."
It turns out that with good design as well as adding extra area, standard apartments can make you amazing.
In fact, you might choose to live, like Joanne.
