The two brothers staged a $500 million real estate empire battle.
Forty years after the founding of its Italian family, the two brothers fought for one of Victoria’s largest real estate empires worth more than $500 million.
Colin De Lutis, a former director and developer of Carleton Football Club, lives in a mansion in Tukala district, while the three gates are located at the residence of his younger brother Paul. The latter took him to the Supreme Court over the division of their real estate and operating assets.
Since 2014, Paul has been hoping to break away from the family business, but the two brothers engaged in a heated debate in the Supreme Court over the division of assets.
The brothers and their mother owned a total of 38 properties worth more than more than $500 million, which have been accumulated since the family bought Smith Street in Collingwood District in 1973.
There, the family founded Westco Jeans and gradually grew to the size of 160 retail chains.
In 1999, the company sold for $85 million.
Since then, the money has been invested in real estate. At present, the portfolio of assets of the De Rutis family includes city office buildings, Braunschweig and Sorento development projects, Gold Coast and Bühler Hill apartments, and suburban shopping centers.
According to an agreement with his late father, Colin, whom his younger brother Paul described as a "principal promoter and dominant figure" of the De Ruttis family, had about two-thirds of the company's assets, compared with about one-third of Paul's.
Documents submitted to the court stated that, according to the commutation, Corin’s possessions were valued at $316 million, compared to $17.6 million owned by Paul.
According to Paul, the division of immovable property and cash proved to be the crux of the negotiations.
“Colin’s proposal to split property left him what I thought was the best,” he wrote in a first court affidavit in 2015.
On Friday, the Supreme Court held its first hearing after several mediation failures.
Now that the brothers are fighting for control of existing businesses, Paul says Colin has kicked him out.
Colin denied this and accused Paul of poor management of certain immovable properties.
In an early affidavit submitted by Collin, he expressed a desire to resolve the dispute.
"My Italian tradition dictates that family is the most important option. My Italian descent determines that family is the most important option," he wrote.
There are hundreds of pages of affidavits, emails, corporate reports submitted by companies such as Deloitte, Pitcher Partners, Charter Keck Cramer, Allan Myers, QC, and senior lawyers hired on both sides.
Ms. de Rutis and her late husband Luigi are both Italian immigrants. They have been operating mass and deli since the late 1950s and bought a shop in Braunschweig in the early 1970s.
They used collateral to buy Collinwood Properties, where Colin opened the family’s first jeans store.
Colin said, “When I’m in high school,” where he founded Westco, “Paul was still very young (about 10 years old).”
Anna de Rutis said that she and Luigi, who died in 2012, want to be able to pass the property to her son in peace.
“Luigi used to say to me that he never wanted his brothers to end up in court,” she wrote.
The other two brothers, Mark and Luke, were no longer members of the de Rutis group of companies, and Colin said that when Mark left the company, he paid him $10 million and $26 million to Luke.
Paul said both Luke and Mark were “dissatisfied with the division clause of their property.”
According to TV star Eddie McGuire’s biography published in 2015, he has kept a low profile, even though Colin is a member of the former AFL club’s board.
“Fairfax Media” contacted Colin and Paul on Friday, along with their lawyers.
However, only Colin replied via text message. He said that because the case has been brought to court, "I really can't say too much."
“It’s very, it’s very, it’s very, very sad that my brother Paul chose to prosecute me and bring our family affairs to court,” he wrote.
In mid-2016, Colin wrote in a court filing that he had tried to meet his brother’s demands.
“Our father doesn’t want to see Paul and me end up in a dispute like this,” he said, adding that he “remains hopeful that the dispute will be resolved.”
On Friday, that hope seemed dashed.
“Our problems will be resolved through the courts in the near future,” Collins wrote.
Li Keqiang held a ceremony to welcome the visit of the Russian Prime Minister to China.
Li Keqiang and the Russian Prime Minister co-chaired the 22nd regular meeting between Chinese and Russian prime ministers
Jointly met with reporters and attended the closing ceremony of the China-Russia Media Exchange Year.
The 30th meeting of the Standing Committee of the 12th National People's Congress held its third plenary meeting.
Zhang Dejiang made a report on the implementation of the law on the prevention and control of solid waste pollution.
Zhang Dejiang met with the Prime Minister of Russia.
