Trump's new chief of staff plans to limit the president's media time
Others have tried, but all of them have failed.
John F. Kelly might actually be able to do it.
If that’s true, he’s the first.
According to Politico, a U.S. political news website, the new White House chief of staff plans to limit the flow of information to President Trump — including news media reports — to keep big bosses more stable.
Here’s a quote from journalist Josh Dawsey:
“When the new White House Chief of Staff, John F. Kelley, began his first day of work to gather discussions with senior officials, he pointed out that he wanted to address one of the main issues with President Trump’s admission to the White House: bad information flowing into the president’s hands.”
Kelley told the officials that receiving good information would help the president make a better decision, so the information would need to go through his hands, both in writing and in the form of briefings.
Kelley’s diagnosis looked likable, but others tried to tame it by spying on Trump’s media time, but it all ended in failure.
President Trump’s TV addiction has been going on for a long time — and now in the White House, he’s habit of watching TV is deepening.
In February, former campaign adviser Samenberg told Politico: "If candidate Trump is frustrated with unfair reporting, it will be of great benefit to let him know that he has also received positive coverage on credible channels."
Politico’s Tara Palmeri wrote at the time, “Based on the experience of six former campaign officials, the focus of controlling Trump’s tweeting habits is to ensure that some praise has always been included in the media messages he personally receives.”
It's completely no problem.
But it’s ridiculous that Trump’s Twitter habits were once “controlled.”
It’s possible that these campaign officials knew something we didn’t know — and that would be even more furious if there hadn’t been for their intervention.
We probably never knew the tweets Trump didn’t send.
If his staff were able to save him from the fire, even if only a few times, their efforts would be worth it.
But no one has been able to stop Trump from causing controversy.
Part of the problem is that in the White House of competing blocs, people promote their worldview by handing out potentially unreliable and unreliable data to Trump.
Politico — all about the story — reported in May that advisers had a penchant for strategically providing suspicious information to the president.
Here’s an example described by journalist Shane Goodmancher:
Current and former Trump officials say Trump’s reactions to negative news segments such as volcanic eruptions, especially those with disruptive information leaks, he’s going crazy to figure out where it came from.
At the end of February, an article on GotNews.com, the website of the cyber instigator Charles C. Johnson, was maliciously copied to the president, which accused Deputy Chief of Staff Katie Walsh of being the “source of a lot of information leaks” in the White House, and that was how Trump behaved at the time.
Even if Johnson has been permanently banned by Twitter for harassment, he has not provided any concrete evidence, and he has blown up a large number of false quality controls, which have since blatantly denied it.
Trump read the article and began asking officials about Walsh’s situation.”
Goodmancher also said that then-chief of staff, Reins Pribas, and White House secretary Rob Porter, had “tried to establish a system to manage and record documents that Trump has received.”
What is the result?
Kelly was going to do the same, in three months.
Maybe he’ll prove that he’s a more efficient manager than Pribas, but Trump’s impulsive Trump, while his aides are still competing against each other for influence.
These eternal influencing factors can make Kelly’s mission even more difficult.
