Motorhomes negotiate the ditch and return to "Rich Street"
Some of the richest owners in the West Midlands are in renewed disputes with determined motorhomers who negotiate a government-excavated ditch to demand the return of land outside the mansion.
RVs parked their 20 trailers in an open space beyond the bustling Solihull Street, which has been nicknamed “Rich Street.”
RVs were carrying horses, holding babies and leading motorhomes, returning after being chased away in May, and the “moat” dug by the municipal authorities did not prevent them from entering.
This sleeky team found that there was a gap in the defensive line, and although there was a part of the road that did not set up roadblocks suitable for passage, their vehicles squeezed through the gap.
Angry residents fiercely criticized the Solihull Council, saying it was “very poor in dealing with the problem.”
One resident, who did not want to be named, said: "They arrived there on Monday night, before they came to Bingrichth Street and threw stones at passers-by."
Commissions should be ashamed of what they have done, because they are simply useless in dealing with problems.
RVs came here in May and were driven away, and now they’re back.
There were a lot of them last night, and there might be more today."
She added: “The Birmingham City Council knows how to deal with it by imposing a long-term ban to protect its parks.
I live on Streetsbrook Road, and if Parliament can’t stop something like this, I wouldn’t be willing to keep paying municipal taxes.
A local resident, who asked not to be named, said: "It's ridiculous, we just saw the last group of RVs gone, it's just convenient for another group to destroy."
This time the number has increased again, and some people have even seen that there are horses eating grass on the lawns of the park.”
“We are aware that there have been unlicenseed camping in an open area of Szützbrook Road, and now we cooperate with Solihull police and take the necessary legal action to get them out as soon as possible.”
In order to support our collection of evidence, it is very important to ask as many local residents as possible to report any anti-social or criminal behavior to the police by calling 101.
This helps us to outline the situation when referring cases to the courts for review by the Court.”
On March 15, the fifth meeting of the 12th National People's Congress held a closing meeting at the Great Hall of the People in Beijing.
This is an interview with the director of the National Postal Service, Ma Junsheng.
