Italy takes a tough stance on NGO migrant rescue boats
Italy issued a warning to non-governmental organizations operating migrant rescue boats in the Mediterranean that they would not be able to continue to implement rescues if they did not sign new rules for the management of their operations.
In an interview with the Turino Daily News, the Minister of the Interior, Marco Minitti, said: “If non-governmental organizations do not sign the new code of conduct, it will be difficult to see why they will continue”.
The warning came a day after Italian officials detained him on suspicion that the crews of the German aid group “Youth Relief” were actually colluding with human traffickers to assist illegal immigrants.
The aid group operated for only one year and declined to comment on the subject of specific allegations by Italian prosecutor Ambrogio Cartosio.
In a social media statement, the organization said: “For us, saving lives is and will be the most important thing in the future, so we regret that we are not currently operating in search and rescue areas.”
“After all the information has been collected, we can only assess all the allegations and the situation at this time.”
Italian officials have been monitoring the Youth Rescue ship Iuventa since October.
Its crew is suspected of carrying human traffickers directly from small boats and allowing smugglers to flee hastily and re-use the ships.
According to prosecutor's documents, at least one such meeting allegedly took place at sea 1.3 miles from the Libyan coast. The contents of the document were published by Italian media.
Its crew is suspected of blatant disregard for the authority of the Italian Coast Guard, which oversees rescue operations in the area out of humanitarian enthusiasm rather than for any other motive.
According to the code of conduct, vessels such as Iuventa clearly required an Italian police officer to supervise their activities on board.
At present, only three of the nine NGOs involved in search-and-rescue operations have agreed to adhere to the code of conduct: Save the Children, the Malta-based migrant maritime assistance station and Pro-Activa Open Arms in Spain.
The latter said on Thursday that it believed the new rules, while unnecessary, were acceptable because they did not bring any “significant changes and obstacles” to their rescue operations.
Among the organizations that refused to sign were the Nobel Prize-winning organization Médecins Sans Frontières (MSF).
At the same time, Minniti said that the number of migrants arriving in Italy has plummeted in recent weeks, indicating that efforts to strengthen Libya's coastal escorts and unite local mayors have begun to bear fruit.
“In recent days, we have begun to see the dawn at the end of the tunnel.”
He unveiled plans for this month’s in-depth talks with Libyan mayors to discuss economic development activities with Chad, Niger and Maly on ways to reduce the number of migrants who want to leave those countries for Europe.
