At 17:10 today, Shaoyang City 119 Command Center received an alarm, and a fire broke out in the tetra-hydrazour stored at the Hongzhi chemical plant located in Longshutang, Shuangqing District of the city.
Shaoyang City Fire Detachment immediately dispatched 5 squadrons of 20 fire trucks carrying heavy anti-chemical clothing, anti-chemical washing trucks and other equipment to the scene.
After preliminary investigation, the fire is a small storage tank for the storage of tetrahydrofurans and other chemical substances, and there is a lot of heavy smoke at the scene.
As of the reporter's press release, the fire officers and soldiers are nervous rescue, the city's health, safety supervision, environmental protection, public security, transportation and other departments have rushed to the scene, did not find casualties.
Michelle Carter was sentenced for texting to encourage Conrad Roy to commit suicide
A Massachusetts woman was sentenced to two-and-a-half years in prison for encouraging her boyfriend to commit suicide via text message and phone.
The 20-year-old Michelle Carter will serve 15 months in prison, after which she can be converted into a suspended sentence of five years.
In June, Carter was convicted of non-deliberate and unpremeditated murder by Conrad Roy, who committed suicide on July 13, 2014.
Roy’s father said at Thursday’s hearing: “My family is heartbroken.
“My son is my best friend.”
While awaiting appeal, Carter was allowed to maintain personal freedom.
She faces up to 20 years in prison, but her group believes she and Mr. Roy are both mentally ill.
At the time of the sentencing, Carter stood, wearing red trousers and a beige t-shirt, with his hands clasped, his eyes in tears, and his eyes facing down.
“Her actions killed Conrad Roy,” prosecutors told the court.
“She ended his life for living better for herself.”
“She wasn’t responsible,” the prosecutor said.
"She doesn't have any regrets."
But Carter's lawyer said it was "a terrible consequence, and she is very sorry."
However, Judge Lawrence Moniz said he did not find that her “age or level of maturity, or even her mental illness, would have any significant impact on her behavior.”
One prosecutor read a statement from Lynn Roy’s mother: “I haven’t had a day without pain in losing my beloved son.
I want him to be proud of what I've done for me. I'm trying to stand with my sisters in all my pain, which will stay with us forever," the statement said.
Mr Roy’s sister, Camdyn Roy, testified that she was “spiritual” because she realized that she could never attend her brother’s wedding or become his child’s aunt.
“It’s painful to lose someone I’ve been with since I’ve been born, and the rest of my life is going to be with that pain,” she said.
This case seems to set a legal precedent, because Massachusetts law does not provide for incentivizing others to commit suicide.
In 2014, Roy was found dead of carbon monoxide poisoning in his car; Carter was 17 years old.
Carter's text messages revealed that the case drew national attention after she instigated him to kill himself.
“There are many ways to hang yourself, jump, stab yourself,” she said in several text messages sent two weeks before his death, when he was on vacation with his family.
In the time before he killed himself, she wrote: “You need to do this, Conrad, “all you have to do is start the generator, and then you’re going to be free and happy.”
Judge Moniz said Carter drew attention through Mr. Roy's death and ordered her not to profit from the case through any media.
There are differences of legal opinion in this case.
Amanda Knox was one of the few who defended Carter, who was acquitted two years after she was accused of murdering her British roommate in Italy in 2007.
Ms. Knox, 30, said in an op-ed published in the Los Angeles Times: “Michelle Carter deserves sympathy and help, not imprisonment.”
