Lavender in Provence is threatened by climate change
Lavender has become a symbol of Provence in southern France, but now there’s bad news that it’s being threatened by climate change.
Lavender is decaying due to ongoing drought and the emergence of virus-carrying insects, leaving scientists and farmers trying to find solutions with headaches.
Eric Chaisse, an expert at the Agricultural Research Institute in Provence (CRIEPPAM), said: “Lavender in Provence is facing a huge threat because the plant has been affected by climate change in recent years, rainfall has decreased significantly in the spring, while in the fall it rains well.
This can affect the life cycle of lavender.
There's also a smaller cicada, a leaf cicadas that can transmit tiny bacteriums, and in very dry weather, they appear in large numbers."
The important thing is that its number is huge.
In addition to attracting vacationers, lavender oil is also sought after by the perfume and cosmetics industry.
Errick Cais said: “Contrary to plants that can open a lot of flowers and have long spikes, there is a problem with smaller individuals, dysplicating, missing, or weak.”
So our essential oil content is less."
Solutions being studied include adopting better irrigation techniques and slowing the evaporation of soil moisture, spraying protective mixtures of clay and water onto plants, and developing more drought-tolerant varieties.
