In Mexico, journalists are killed.
All of Riodoce’s weekly employees usually meet on Wednesday to review their plans to report on the Sinaloa riots caused by criminal organizations, corrupt officials, and an endless war on drugs.
But today, the shadow of the tragedy hangs over them, who come together to discuss security.
It’s important that they’re told to change their daily habits.
Two senior journalists discussed what made them feel safer: whether to take their children to the office, which had been attacked with a grenade in 2009, or to keep them at home.
The security expert wrote three words on the blackboard in front of the room: enemies, neutrals, and allies.
They let these reporters come up with the following names in each column—no need for evidence, observation and intuition is enough.
There are suspected drug dealers, politicians, businessmen and journalists on government lists, or cartels, which have made Mexico’s reporting work dangerous.
The violence has never stopped, and there have been more and more journalists: according to the Committee to Protect Journalists, at least 25 journalists have been killed since President Enrique Peña Nieto took office in December 2012; 589 journalists have come under federal protection after being attacked and threatened; and so far this year, at least seven journalists have died in seven states.
The last to die was their editor and source of inspiration, as well as the co-founder of Riodoce, Javier Valdez Cárdenas.
Valdez has written many books on drug violence, in one of which he wrote: “The biggest mistake is to live in Mexico and become a journalist.”
Everyone can feel deeply that he’s gone, even though he’s everywhere – a huge picture hanging on the front of the Riodoce office building, with Valdez’s middle finger, the word “righteousness”; Aaron Öwara and Miriam Ramirez, two journalists in their 30s wearing T-shirts that also display his smile, he’s wearing glasses or his iconic Panama hat.
Less than two months after his death, the seminar was held; journalists discussed their shared traumas, nightmares, insomnia, and suspicions.
Although a special federal prosecutor’s office was set up in 2010 to deal with journalists’ cases, it prosecuted only two people, according to the Committee for the Protection of Journalists’ records.
Most of the thousands of criminal cases each year involve drug trafficking and organized crime, so it is rarely possible to bring to justice the perpetrators of the killings of journalists.
In Riodoce, they insist on covering the violence in Sinaro, even though they are heartbroken, even though the land is porous.
Öbara said there was no information about the killers and no access to justice, so it wouldn’t be too useful to hold a security seminar.
“It’s silly to waste time attending seminars like this.
As long as we don't know why, you don't trust anyone."
On the morning of May 15, Valdez left his Riodoce office in Culiacan, the capital.
His red Toyota Corolla drove only a few blocks away and was stopped by two men; they forced him to get out of the car and fired 12 shots, guessing because of the name of the newspaper—translated as "Twelve Rivers."
The gangster drove out of his car and knocked it down nearby.
Vardez is 50 years old.
Leaving his wife and two grown-up children.
Culiakan gossiped four times.
But there’s actually nothing but silence about Valdez’s topic.
Since Guzman was arrested last year and extradited to the United States in January, Sinalo has become the country's bloodiest battleground, with hostile forces frantically fighting.
One or several people have been shot every day on the streets of Sinalo, and the cemetery is littered with two-story mausoleums filled with drug lords, which have become commonplace.
To forget for a moment the “calm” state is when only a cartel controls this coastal state and its ports to the United States and drug trafficking routes.
Even so, even though Valdez knew the dangers of the job, 60-year-old Riodoce co-founder and director Ismael Pohokui still felt guilty because he didn’t protect his friends.
He believed that two mistakes had caused the tragedy.
First, an interview with Damaso Lopez was published in February, in which he led a cartel fighting several of Guzman’s sons.
This report may have angered the sons; as soon as the newspaper was sent to the newsstand, it was bought out by suspected gang members.
The second mistake was that another newspaper reporting the same story had been confiscated without forcing Valdez to leave Mexico for his own safety.
Valdez is a legend in Mexico and abroad, and his death is of great significance to Mexico’s violence against journalists.
His spirit endures, and his friends and colleagues believe that he has always been with us.
In 2003, he was also a senior reporter for Noroeste, when he co-founded Riodoce with five colleagues and sold a $50 stake.
In Sinarro, Pohokuui said, “it’s not possible to get involved in journalism without dealing with drugs.”
With the passage of time, the newspaper has gained a good reputation for bold and honest reporting, and sales and advertising have also increased.
Reporters like to be able to publish investigation reports that directly attack the key without worrying about censorship, and readers also like newspapers to publish stories that no one else is afraid to report.
Five years after its inception, Riodoce won the Maria Moscapo Prize for Journalism in Latin America.
In the same year, the Society for the Protection of Journalists awarded the Valdez International Press Freedom Prize for his courage in the pursuit of the most authentic Mexican drug reporting.
He admitted that he was also scared.
When the Society for the Protection of Journalists awarded the award, he said, “I want to keep living.”
Later, in late October, he said in an interview on the local television station Rompeviento TV that “in Sinalo, drug trafficking is a way of life.”
You’ve got to take on the task that’s on your shoulders as a journalist – either that’s the way it’s, or it’s deaf and dumb.
“I don’t want to be asked, ‘What have you done in the face of so many deaths... why don’t you speak out about what’s going on?’”
"Riodoce" all the staff miss Vardez, miss him like a dock worker, miss this friend who has never regretted hugging and advice, miss this teacher who knows how to live.
It depends on his habits.
In the past, he used to wear that hat.
The El Guayabo bar, which is often opposite the office, is always sitting at the same table.
Now, they’re going to ask: Was it his daily habit that caused his fall?
His death also made them have to think about how to do a good job and live well.
“They don’t like the women and kids you’re in trouble with, and they don’t like the secret airports where you affect their legitimate companies and those used to transport drugs.”
All of this crossed their bottom line.”
As a result, a high degree of self-censorship and self-preservation should be maintained, even at the safest time.
Believe in your instincts.
If you sniff the dangerous breath, stay away.
Riodoce editor Andrés Villarier said that the problem is that “breath is a feeling of being cheated . . and then something like Havel will happen.”
He and others say that previous rules no longer apply to Sinaro—just as they no longer apply to Tamaulipas, Veracruz, Guerrero and other states, where smuggling routes are lucrative, while agencies are petty, government officials are corrupt.
The situation is constantly changing.
Mobile phones were suddenly not allowed to be used by Riodoce employees in a room for security training; a few days ago, spyware allegedly sold only to the government had been used in Mexico to spy on journalists and activists.
Outside, two police officers cooled in the shade, sheltering 104 degrees (40 degrees Celsius).
They were sent by the state government to protect Riodoce’s offices, which are housed in a four-storey building in a middle-class neighborhood in Culiyakan.
Some reporters joked half jokingly that they didn’t know if these police officers would belong to 50% of what the governor himself said.
It’s been a few months since the journalists went to the mountains, where drug cartels are under de facto control.
Riodoce this week covered three major events.
Among them was the killing of the brothers of the great former boxer Julio César Chávez of Sinaro,
There is also the disclosure of government funds in the governor's hometown,
As well as the kidnapping inside an expensive restaurant in Culiakan, which is only a block from the prosecutor’s office.
The authorities did not release information about those who had been kidnapped and how they had been kidnapped, so it was necessary to be careful when writing about what the city knew: the restaurant was where both drug dealers and officials loved to go.
A reporter learned from public records that the restaurant was registered in the name of a politician of the ruling Revolutionary Institutional Party (PRI), which dominated the political strata almost throughout the previous period.
More recently, the governors of several revolutionaries have been accused of embezzlement in several highly regarded cases.
Villarier asked reporters to write about what had happened at the site of the incident, including one of Mexico's top drug lords, Guzman's son, sheltered from a military attack.
A few months ago, readers would check out Valdez’s column to look for the most powerful information about the kidnapping.
The 46-year-old Villarier, known as a “skinny man” because of his thin figure, said “before, we already knew what was going on.”
Now all our sources of communication have been cut off.
Valdez’s office is now being used to store signs and stickers protesting the killing of journalists, as employees have become what they never wanted to be: activists representing the media.
The next morning, the journalist Miriam Ramirez grabbed a sign and walked out of the room and went to the local prosecutor’s office to take part in a march related to another journalist.
Salvador Adame disappeared three days after Vardez was killed in the western state of Michoacán.
Officials found a charred body and said it was based on DNA testing, which was his body.
Across the country, journalists have become more outspoken, organizing protests, painting the words “rescue the media” on sidewalks and buildings.
On this day, director Pohokui is not in Washington, and he is trying to get justice and international support for the Valdez case.
The day after Valdez was killed, during a meeting with the governor of Sinaro, Ramirez accused the authorities of spying on the reporter's message and killing them because they had told the truth.
Given that her anger had affected her objectivity, she also asked to step down from reporting on government affairs.
Valdez has repeatedly reiterated that journalists in Mexico are “encircled” by organized crime, complicated government officials, and indifferent societies.
In his previous book, Drug Journalism, he wrote that journalists were not only mutilated by drug cartels, but were also on the list of politicians and security forces complicated with organized crime.
Articulo 19, a media watchdog group, blamed police and public officials for more than half of last year’s journalist attacks.
Ramirez said, “In Mexico, they let you die in order for you to shut up.”
Obviously, these murders are chilling.
No one forgets that six years ago, the blogger Maria Elisabeth Marcias died on the northern border of Tamaulipas.
Next to her body is a note purportedly signed by the Zetas cartel: “I have come for my reporting.”
Her head was cut off with a computer keyboard and headphones next to it.
After Miroslava Brechi was killed in March, some newspapers chose to shut down, such as El Norte newspaper on the northern border of Chihuahua.
Others are still going on, with directors of El Manana of Nuevo Laredo being killed in 2014, but they’re still going on.
In 2010, Diario de Ciudad Juarez spoke publicly with a front-page news about the drug cartel, with the title “What do you want from us?”
Some of the journalists fled their hometowns, or even left the country.
This is a difficult choice.
It’s hard to find jobs in exile, and they still need to be cautious and wary of danger on the streets.
Sometimes they would be arrested, and in 2015 photographer Ruben Espinosa was said to have been killed in a Mexican city along with four women three months after fleeing Veracruz.
For those staying behind the scenes and continuing to work, it’s a day-to-day walk for high-risk decisions.
Iwara, who wanted to become a poet, admitted that reporting on the drug trade terrified him.
“Mexico is going to hell, so I’ve become a journalist,” he said.
In the middle of the middle of the most recent Friday, the latest issue of the newspaper had been printed, and the editors of Riodoce were sitting on the aisle outside their offices drinking beers, and suddenly their phones were ringing.
Nineteen people have been confirmed dead after a series of gunfights and security forces near the beach resort city of Mazatlán.
The fighting is still escalating, as foreshadowed by a range of cartel information found in the region.
They uploaded the news to Riodoce’s website just on the side of the road through their mobile phones.
The next day’s headline had to be changed.
The sirens sounded nearby – there was yet another duel in the area.
Pohokui glanced at the policeman standing there guarding, to see if they were alert.
If they’re a little scared, then they’ren’t showing it either.
The founder of the newspaper had been killed, and on his large portrait, his middle finger pointed to the whole of Sinalo that needed to look, and the entire staff of Riodoce followed in his footsteps.
“How can you even want to shut it down?” said Bohokui, who was killed on the same day when the intern asked me to send her into the street to participate in the reporting.”
China's self-driving car tourism market in 2020 may exceed one trillion yuan
In the context of consumption upgrading and the prevalence of free travel, self-driving travel is encouraged by national policies and more and more consumers.
However, at the same time, the self-driving tourism market, which is expected to break through the trillion scale, is also facing double challenges.
In the context of consumption upgrading and the prevalence of freedom, self-driving tourism is encouraged by national policies and more and more consumers.
However, at the same time, it is expected to break through the trillion-scale self-driving tourism market is also facing double challenges, on the one hand, the development of self-driving travel industry is not proportional to the market demand, self-driving travel clubs and travel agencies and other self-driving tour organizers are facing the dilemma of small profits; on the other hand, the infrastructure and services faced by self-driving travel are still in their primary stages.
It accounts for 60% of the total number of domestic tourists, and the market size is expected to break the trillion.
In 2016, 4.44 billion domestic trips, self-driving tourism accounted for about 60% of the total number of domestic tourists, and by 2020, the scale of self-driving tourism market is expected to exceed trillions.
According to the statistics of the China Tourism Vehicle Association on the number of self-driving trips, in the past five years, the number of people from driving has continued to grow, and the proportion of domestic tourists has stabilized at more than half.
At the same time, regional and national levels have shown more attention and support for self-driving travel in this emerging area, and from 2016 to 2017, policies and regulations related to self-driving cars, sojourning vehicles and camping tourism were intensively introduced.
In July 2017, eight departments such as the National Tourism Administration and the General Administration of Sports jointly issued the "Car Self-Driving Camp Development Plan"; in the same month, 14 departments such as the National Development and Reform Commission and the Ministry of Finance jointly issued the Action Plan for Promoting the Development of Rural Tourism to Improve the Quality and Upgrading (2017).
The introduction of policies has provided opportunities and policy guidance for the development of domestic self-driving tourism.
The self-driving travel club's profitability is meager, and the complete industrial chain is the key.
Although the domestic self-driving tourism market has great potential and national policies are actively guided, the self-driving tourism industry is still in its infancy.
According to the annual development report of China's self-driving travel in 2016-2017, there are thousands of domestic self-driving travel clubs, car clubs, and riders' clubs, but the proportion of self-driving trips for tourists accounts for the majority, and the proportion of tourists participating in various institutions such as ride-hailing clubs is only about 15%.
At the same time, the independent research and development of self-driving travel products of online travel enterprises and traditional travel agencies are also a handful, many online tourism platforms sell only some of the suppliers of self-driving tourism products, some self-driving travel clubs are also important suppliers.
From the perspective of organizing self-driving travel activities, it covers many aspects of "eating, living, traveling, traveling, shopping, and entertainment", the degree of fragmentation is high, the upstream supply chain is heavy, and the randomness of tourist consumption is large.
Li Guanjie, founder and CEO of Beijing Xingtian Automobile Club, believes that self-driving travel clubs are essentially similar to travel agencies, and the main profit models in the early days are dominated by offline activities, and the core point is self-driving travel line products and leadership services.
However, today, the homogenization of line products in the field of self-driving travel is serious, and the level of leader service is uneven, and self-driving travel clubs are faced with the need for another transformation.
It can be seen that self-driving travel itself is not an independent industry, but with the displacement characteristics of self-driving travel has formed an ecological circle across regions and industries, and it is very important to form a complete industrial chain.
Experts pointed out that in addition to basic tourism consumption, experience is a more important consumption element.
Therefore, for the development of the self-driving tourism industry, it is necessary to enhance the characteristics of the experience and develop the theme of "self-driving tour +" as well as the IP of self-driving travel products.
Scenic roads are not only roads, car camps should not only meet accommodation.
In the face of the rise of self-driving travel, the basic supporting development of self-driving travel is also relatively primary.
Li Guanjie pointed out that the tourist scenic road is not just a road, but it is necessary to integrate the elements of transportation and tourism, but the construction of roads and tourism itself are responsible for two departments, the transportation department to increase the tourism elements, but also stay in the construction of toilets, consulting centers, publicity columns and other facilities, and the combination with tourism is not high.
Compared with the well-known self-driving route "American Route 66", the construction experience is worthy of reference.
Scenic design is no longer the most direct and fast path between the place of departure to the destination, but follows the design concept of "experiencing driving fun" to provide self-driving tourists with the best enjoyment of the beautiful scenery along the road; the scenic road can connect the point-shaped and surface-like tourism landscape, both retaining the historical value and cultural value of eco-tourism resources along the route, but also holding a variety of thematic activities to enhance the participation and leisure experience of tourists and create economic benefits for the local tourism industry.
In addition to the scenic path, the camp is also an important supporting facility for self-driving tours.
However, among the self-driving tourists that the Beijing News reporter understands, there are not many consumers who choose to stay in the camp.
They believe that many camps are biased towards infrastructure and can only meet the needs of eating and living, the grade is not high, and the product is single, which is unable to meet the needs of self-driving travel, which contains many elements.
Some industry insiders who did not want to disclose their names said that many of the enterprises that did the camp in the past few years were to take land, and after taking land, the supporting infrastructure construction and services in the later period did not keep up, and many of the operating conditions were losses, and there was no later capital injection, and it was difficult to do some corresponding supporting products.
Category: Consumpising groups
According to the data released by the China Tourism Vehicle Association in 2017, from the perspective of age, self-driving travel groups are concentrated in 21-40 years old, especially between 31 and 40 years old, which has a certain economic foundation, and the demand for personalized tourism is high, and most of them are parent-child travel customers.
Judging from the accommodation survey, self-driving tourists have a relative preference for non-standard accommodation.
Judging from the type of self-driving travel, the surrounding self-driving is the main type of self-driving travel, accounting for 84.32%.
Driving abroad is the yearning of many people, but the proportion is still relatively small.
At the same time, with the improvement of consumer demand, tourists who used to like to drive "tour attractions" began to change their concepts, no longer in the process of self-driving travel, "take pictures, get in the car to sleep", but pay more attention to the experience and process of travel.
Dinosaurs used camouflage to avoid the enemy
At first glance, it’s terrible, but it looks like a statue.
The researchers released the first study of an inconceivable biofossil the size of a rhino found by Canadian miners, pointing out that the fossil is well-preserved, so experts refer to it as the “Mona Lisa of the Dinosaur World.”
The fossil is so well preserved that it can even conclude that its squamous skin is reddish-brown. This suggests that it has had some "annoying experiences."
Reports in the journal Current Biology describe it as "one of the best-preserved dinosaurs ever found and one of the best dinosaur specimens in the world."
The fossil is a newly discovered species that lived between 110 and 11.2 million years ago during the Cretaceous period.
It’s extremely well-preserved and shocked researchers, who described it as “really breathtaking.”
According to National Geographic, this herbivorous dinosaur fossil is the best-preserved dinosaur fossil ever found.
On March 21, 2011, Shawn Funk discovered the fossil while mining at the Senkel Millennium Mine near Fort McMurray in northern Alberta, Canada.
At that time, he encountered something that didn’t seem like the rocks around him, so he decided to take a closer look.
After that, the fossils he found were sent to the Royal Tyrrell Museum of Antiquities in Canada.
It took six years for the researchers to bring out the beast inside the 2,500-pound (1,100-kilogram) cland.
After so much hard work, the final results have only now been announced.
"If you look at it, you're almost sure it's going to sleep," said Caleb Brown, a scientist at the Royal Tyrell Museum.
“It will become one of the most beautiful and well-preserved dinosaur specimens in the history of science – the Mona Lisa in the Dinosaur world.”
By studying its skin, the researchers found that the herbivorous creature, although dressed in armor, like a walking tank, is likely to receive a major threat from carnivorous dinosaurs.
Dinosaurs wearing heavy armor used red and white camouflages to avoid predators, and used concealment known as "anti-shadowing", which is also used by many modern animals.
Experts say that in this way, it is better able to blend in with its surroundings, thereby avoiding enemies, indicating that it is preyed upon by larger dinosaurs.
The 110-million-year-old creature, who is part of a family that made headlines for the first time in May, is currently on display at the Royal Tyrrell Museum.
Mark Mitchell, one of the team’s museum technicians, took 7,000 hours to delicately lift the behemoth’s veil, and the researchers now studied it and named it “Borealopelta Markmitchelli.”
The samples are well preserved, allowing Dr. Mitchell, as well as an international team of scientists, to record the patterns and shapes of scales and armor on their bodies.
They discovered “anti-shadeing,” a common form of defense in the animal kingdom, meaning that the creature’s back is darker than the color on its sides.
Although "anti-shadowing" surgery is common, the results found are surprising, because the dinosaur's size far exceeds that of existing animals using anti-shading.
This dinosaur faced the pressure of being hunted by meat-eating dinosaurs, thus evolving in a favorable direction, allowing it to be hidden in the strait as a means of survival.
However, the fact that most of the animals in existence that use anti-shading, such as deer, zebras, or armadillons, are as small and fragile as prey, suggesting that the creature is facing real pressure to survive.
“The frenzied predation of large heavy armor dinosaurs shows how dangerous Cretaceous carnivorous dinosaurs were,” Brown said.
Chemical analysis of organic complexes in their scales also indicates that the dinosaur’s skin color was red and brown.
Scientists are continuing to study the creature’s clues to life, including its retained stomach content, to find out what was finally eaten.
They believed that the dinosaurs fell into the river at the time of death, then washed into the sea, and finally sank to the bottom of the sea in the way of falling down.
At the time, Alberta’s climate was as warm as it is today in southern Florida, and rivers and oceans may be deeper inland than they are now.
Brown said: “This nebulation dragon is truly amazing, because it is completely covered with preserved squamous skin, and, so far, it has been preserved in dimensional space, so that its original shape can be preserved.”
Therefore, the creature is now in almost the same shape as it was during the Cretaceous period.
You don't have to use too much imagination to rebuild it; if you look at it, you'll almost believe it's sleeping."
During the Cretaceous period, it was a time when two-legged large theropod dinosaurs roamed the earth.
Although Tyrannosaurus rex was the king of dinosaurs, it appeared millions of years after the draped dinosaurs. The drapes dinosaurs may have been hunted up by some of their ancestors.
They include high thorny dragons, a behemoth weighing up to 6 tons and 38 feet (11.5 meters) long.
The latest study found that the scientists who appeared in the journal Current Biology believe that the knot dragon was washed into the sea after death, and later formed mummies in the soil.
On March 21, 20101, Shaun Fink excavated the creature at the Millennium Mine near Fort McMurray in Alberta, Canada.
According to the museum, it is the world's best-preserved drape dinosaur, and the skin and armour in the area from the nose to the buttock are intact.
On average, this creature is about 18 feet (5 meters) long and weighs 3,000 pounds (1,300 kilograms).
It has two 20-inch-long angles protruding from the shoulders.
The researchers believe that this herbivorous dragon is active in what is now western Canada, and then washed into the sea by a flooded river.
However, this dinosaur's burial at the bottom of the sea made it possible to preserve its armor in its entirety.
The fossil remains of this particular sample are very well preserved, leaving the remaining skin still covered with uneven slices on the dinosaur skull.
As Michael Greshko wrote in National Geographic, this level of preservation is as rare as it is.
The more I looked, the more I thought it was unbelievable.
Residual skin fossils are still covered in mottled armour on a dinosaur’s skull.
Its right front palm is located on its side, with five toes unfolding upwards.
I can count them."
