Environmentally friendly, the potential for increased crop production is obvious, especially large particle value-added urea is a high-quality raw material for BB fertilizer.
The total number of security personnel is double that of the 2012 London Olympics.
In order to ensure the safety of the Rio Olympic Games, Brazil will launch the largest security linkage plan in history.
Earlier, Brazil's government said that a total of 85,000 people from the army and police will work together, including 20,000 soldiers and 65,000 police officers.
Most military personnel will be responsible for guarding Olympic stadiums, while the new 3,000 will be responsible for the security of international airports, subway stations and streets.
Polices patrol other parts of the city, including subways, buses and streets.
It is reported that more than 6,000 military personnel have already arrived in Rio for Olympic security services.
In addition to being in charge of the four major areas of the Rio Olympic Games, they are also responsible for patrolling the airport, as well as on the main roads connecting the airport and Olympic venues.
The introduction of evaluation mechanisms in the service industry is no longer new, in order to make the evaluation mechanism effective, it is important to make the supervision more effective, not in form.
How radicalists learned to answer the question: “Whose streets?”
On August 9, 2014, Brittany Ferrer was troubled.
On the first day of arriving in New York City, the St. Louis man sat on his rental bed and browsed Facebook on his phone.
She stumbled upon the post of a high school student.
“Police have just killed an 18-year-old child who is still lying in the street.”
She was puzzled to find that no one else had posted the message on the feed. She turned off Facebook and opened Twitter.
A @TheePharoah user kept pushing her the dynamic Times.
St. Louis-area rappers tweet about Michael Brown's death. Michael Brown, an unarmed 18-year-old black man, was killed by a white police officer in Ferguson, Missouri.
He posted a picture of Brown's body lying on the street where he had lost his vital signs, and his body lay in the street for four hours.
"It's crazy," Ferrer thought.
But death is nothing new for her and her community.
She put down her phone and didn’t open Twitter again until the evening of that night, reading tweets about Canfield Avenue.
There were police footage and pictures of people shouting, as well as a picture of someone claiming to be Brown’s father, which read: “Ferguson police have just killed my unarmed son!”
She saw a message at the scene where a police officer stood in front of a group of protesters with a barking dog.
She said that in another video, another policeman raised his rifle and screamed: "I'm going to kill all of you ... animals."
“It wasn’t normal,” Ferrer recalled.
“It’s not something we’re used to, it’s supposed to be a typical murder.”
At that moment, I shuddered.
I'm angry, I'm crying."
She booked a return flight and went home the next day.
After landing at 11 p.m., she picked up her six-year-old daughter from her father’s house and decided to travel to Ferguson the next morning.
For the next three years, what happened to Ferrer and Ferguson was the subject of a new documentary, “Who’s the Street?”, which was released in theaters on August 11.
It’s a story about survival and protest, love and loss, strength, and resilience from the perspective of the people who live in this community, crowding the streets demanding answers.
When Ferrer and his daughter (wearing a floral dress, with crown accessories) driving to Ferguson, they re-discussed the countless topics they had discussed before that morning—“about the black experience and the condition of the Negro.”
“Do you remember when I taught you that black people had to fight for their faith?” she recalled.
“We’re going to Ferguson now because the police killed an 18-year-old boy, and that’s not right.
“I had to take her,” Ferrer said later.
“What’s going on today is an climax of everything that’s in the past.
She wants to know, see, and rise from it.
Not only does she need to develop well in terms of radicalism and organization, but also to understand the different aspects of the black experience and the situation of black people, to understand what we have to do in order to be free and liberated.
Even though I was fortunate enough to provide her with racist experiences she hadn’t experienced personally, she wasn’t any different from other black girls.
“If I protect her in this matter, I feel like I’m hurting her.”
They arrived at Ferguson's first stop at a site across the street from the police station of South Florida Road. The militants became their camp by the result of police officer Darren Wilson's eventual non-prosecution.
Ferrer and her daughter are in touch with a local business owner who makes a bagged lunch.
They prepared the bags and were responsible for passing them to the protesters.
Gradually, Ferrer began to form a new kind of activism.
“My radicalism changed completely after August 9,” she says, when she enrolled at Missouri State University’s campus in St. Louis, she was president of the Society of Minority Nurses, focusing on food justice and health disparities in black communities.
“I don’t have organizational experience.
That’s how it all happened.”
A few weeks later, Ferrer met the film's co-director, Sabah Foleyang, during a nocturnal parade.
Foleyan said she and her photography director, Lucas Alvarado Faler, "just wanted to document" what had happened.
Ferrer’s mind came up with the question: “Do you want to record or do you want to find a story that you can take advantage of?”
This skepticism stems from many of these cases in which people — often white people — enter a community to benefit from their pain and tenacity.
As Fleyang explained in a recent interview, “We’ve heard that natives of Ferguson aren’t focusing on their voices, and we want to do it differently.”
Foleyan has been in contact with co-director Dimon Davis. Dimon Davis is a regional artist known for his activism against death row inmates.
His involvement allowed Ferrer, along with six other locals, to join the “Who’s the Streets?” project team, a few years after Brown’s shooting.
“This documentary is not about someone speaking for us or talking to us, it’s what we’re talking about,” Davis said.
“I’m mostly focused on how these characters are presented, because that’s how I’m presented.”
Although the goal is not to focus on Ferrer’s experience – but to show that the movement “isn’t about savior leaders, it’s about community cohesion,” Foleyan said – it turns out she’s the most open for the film’s participants.
As a result, “Whose Street?” surprisingly recorded Ferrer’s personal life, such as falling in love and getting married.
“I feel naked,” Ferrer laughed the first time he saw the finished product.
“But I understand that to do the job and humanize black people, there has to be a certain level of vulnerability.”
In addition, Ferrer’s story reaffirms and repositions the roles of black queer women in the broader Black Life events movement.
Two of the movement’s three founders — who created #BlackLivesMatter on social media in the wake of the 2012 shooting of black teen Trevorn Martin — were identified as queuers.
“Black queer women in the leadership supported this movement in general,” Ferrer said, “because we’re constantly fighting on multiple fronts.”
Black queer women have to endure it all."
Foleyan agreed and mentioned that “this movement was really supported by black women and many black queer women”.
Because of her identity, Ferrer was selected as the centerpiece of the film. However, she insisted that "this is not an affirmative action for things".
“She’s the one who inspires this kind of energy,” she said.
"Although she's on the front lines, it's not a coincidence. Because in life at these crossroads, as a black queer woman, you have a lot to do.
Through the screen, you can feel her energy."
At the same time, in Ferguson, news cameras have long since disappeared, but tensions between communities and the police still exist.
Ferrer was sentenced to more than two-and-a-half years of probation following an attempt to block the highway during a parade.
She describes the socio-political atmosphere in the area she calls home: “a mess.”
Nevertheless, since taking to the streets of Ferguson three years ago, she’s stuck to the hymns she’s struggled to remember.
“It’s our responsibility to fight for freedom.
It is our responsibility to win.
We have to love each other to support each other.
We can't lose anything, except for the shackles on our bodies."
In fact, online translation services have been provided on the Internet a decade ago, but in fact, a long time ago, the tone of "machines are about to replace human translation" is endless.
Similarities: The artist creates portraits using Chelsea Manning’s DNA.
There are multiple faces in the middle of the exhibition hall at New York’s Friedman Art Gallery: white, black, and brown, with almost imperceptible similarities between each face, which are tied by ropes hanging down from the ceiling.
These 30 portraits were created by the artist Heather Dewey-Hagborg using Chelsea Manning to send her cheek cotton swabs and hair crumbs.
Manning sent bruised hair from Livenburg prison for leaking classified diplomatic cables through WikiLeaks, where the former intelligence analyst was sentenced to 35 years in prison.
The exhibition opened on August 2 with the title “Simlities.”
This is the result of two years of communication between Dewey and Manning, who combined his discipline with his own technology, computer science, and art, while Manning, a well-known dissident, went to prison, where he completed gender reassignment surgery. Former U.S. President Barack Obama pardoned her remaining sentence three days before the end of his term.
Through algorithmic analysis of Manning's DNA, he created 30 portraits of "probable appearances". Heacer Duwey Harberg made a sharp and slightly unrelenting comment. His comments were both porous about DNA and the concept of identity itself.
Manning, who was known as Bradley Edward when he was born, publicly admitted to identifying herself as a woman in adolescence. He was convicted in 2013 of 20 charges, including six counts of spying violations, computer fraud, and theft.
In prison, her image is suppressed, with only one photo, a black-and-white selfie with a granular feel, looking noticeably restless, sitting in the driver’s seat, wearing a platinum wig.
In 2015, Heather Duwey Harberg was a great success in her 2012 project, Stranger Visions.
In that work, she used forensics from the smoking tries and chewing gum middle courts, DNA extracted from crumbs, to create 3D portraits that might look similar.
That’s when she received an email from Paper magazine.
“They wanted to interview Chelsea Manning in prison and needed a portrait,” Heather Duwey Harberg told the media at the opening of the exhibition.
Chelsea Manning couldn’t visit at the time and couldn’t take pictures, so they contacted Chelsea to ask if she was interested in making DNA portraits.
As a result, the artist and her incarcerated muse became an improbable pen pal, exchanging many letters with each other for two years.
They even created a comic book, The Repressing Avatar, by Shoili Kanungo . The comic book foresees that the president can grant Manning privileges in the future, and that she can see the exhibition in prison.
Obama asked to be released just days after the book's publication. This may be a coincidence, when Manning spent his seventh year at the Kansas military prison.
In addition, there is another depth to this exhibition. Because in the contemporary United States, the Trump administration continues to declare war on government leakers and transgender soldiers, while looking for new ways to hype on Twitter to nakedly incite division, and this exhibition is about bridging this division.
When asked about the special timing of all of this, Heather Duwey Harberg said, “There’s a reason for anything to happen.”
In an artist’s statement written on the gallery’s wall, Manning’s oversized signature is below (the “i” in the name is drawn with one heart) calling for an end to the “partial strife on the basis of gender, sexual orientation, and culture.”
On the opposite wall is her mitochondrial DNA sequence, twisted with a pencil with Cs, Gs, and As symbols.
By containing 200 letters (or nucleotides, each artist’s complex scientific dictionary), Heather Duwey Harberg hopes to express that, at least in terms of physiological structures, this is the case for everyone by depicting these surprisingly similar portraits.
“I want people to get out of it, and our genome doesn’t care who we are, how public genetic data is interpreted, and how subjective it is,” said Heather Duwey Harberg, whose 2007 video, False Memory, is also on display.
“DNA data can tell us many different stories, so that’s 30 of them.”
The last work in the exhibition, in one room, comes from one page in the novella in the previous illustration.
Manning, which shows soldiers in the United States holding loudspeakers under the pompous, King Kong styling.
“When they made your speech stop, they won,” the wall wrote.
"Don't shut up."
“It’s directly from a letter she wrote to me,” the Philadelphia-based artist said.
“I’m scared, and I’m still going to say it.”
Manning hasn’t seen the exhibition in person, as foreseen in the comics, but when she does, she’ll be greeted by her own DNA portrait mask, just as a group of protesters were cheered in the center of the pavilion.
It’s also purposeful, just like all the objects in the exhibition, reaching the height of the genetic halogroup information she wrote in a pencil (5.2 feet, Manning’s height).
“In the past, I hung them against the wall, but I wanted them to feel like a group of people,” Dewey Hargerburg said of the portrait.
“But I wanted to make them feel like a bunch of protesters following Chelsea, just as there were people protesting to release her.”
Northwestern University professor and Oxford University employee wanted for homicide
Police said they found a man fatally stabbed inside a luxury apartment in Chicago, and a Northwestern University professor and an Oxford University employee were subsequently wanted and are currently looking for suspects across the country.
According to Cook County court records on Monday, 42-year-old Winderham Lessheim and 56-year-old Andrew Warren were arrested on a first-degree unbaissed murder warrant in connection with the death of Trenton H. James Duranlow.
According to documents, 26-year-old Cornell Duranlow died from multiple stabbings.
According to a community alert issued by the Chicago Police Department, the body was found on July 27.
After receiving the call, police arrived at the Grand Plaza apartment and found that there had been multiple ruptures on the victim's body.
According to police, Cornell Duranlow died on the spot, and detectives are investigating the murder, and police are actively looking for two suspects.
Lessham has been an associate professor of microbiology and immunology at Northwestern University since 2007.
Alan K. Kobech, deputy director of campus relations, said in an emailed statement that Lesem has now left and has been barred from the Northwestern University campus.
“It is now a criminal case and the relevant authorities are investigating, and Northwestern University is actively cooperating with the investigation.”
Warren is a senior finance assistant at the University of Oxford.
In an emailed statement, a spokesman for Somerville College at the University of Oxford said: "We have been in touch with the British police and stand ready to assist the U.S. investigative body as needed."
Warren’s colleagues were deeply shocked and wanted him to turn himself in, the statement added.
Chicago Police Chief Liaison Officer Anthony Gullemi expressed strong encouragement for the suspect to turn himself in from the authorities.
“Police already have an approximate idea of their whereabouts, and we are trying to locate possible locations,” Gurlemi said.
Guremi said that local, regional and national authorities are doing their utmost to assist in the search for suspects.
Gurlemi said police had received a call from the manager of Grand Plaza, who had just received a secret message that something had happened in apartment 1008.
Police said the situation was terrible at the time.
Guremi said it was still unclear whether the incident also took place on July 27.
Based on security cameras, he said it was confirmed that Lessem and Warren were in the building at the time.
“We are confident that Professor Lessham has a certain relationship with the victims,” Gulermi said.
Grand Plaza’s management team issued a statement to residents: “Police are currently studying the victim’s timeline and background, and are investigating a variety of motivations, including a possible domestic incident.”
Russian Deputy Prime Minister Mutko said that "we will complete the roadmap of the World Anti-Doping Agency in the near future".
Russian Deputy Prime Minister Vitaly Mutko said it would not take too long to implement the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA’s) remaining standards for the restoration of the rights of the Russian Anti-Doping Agency (RUSADA).
“Implementation of the roadmap criteria has reached a certain stage, and RUSADA has acquired the right to work with UKAD (Anti-Doping Agency) for planning and testing.
The WADA roadmap has been updated in this regard,” Mutko said on Thursday.
Deputy Prime Minister Mutko made the remarks when commenting on Part II of WADA's road map, Compliance Regulations, which sets out the criteria for the restoration of RUSADA's rights, which was recently posted on the organization's website.
According to Mutko, the standard does not contain anything that could cause concern to Russia.
He added, “Everything has to be announced and everything will be implemented.”
The results will also be presented to the International Olympic Committee [IOC] and WADA in the near future.”
However, one of the criteria mentioned in the list is that the Russian Anti-Doping Agency must publicly accept the findings approved by Canadian lawyer Richard McLaren on WADA’s support for doping incidents in domestic sports.
Referring to the matter, Mutko said: "We are investigating and there is no doubt that our anti-doping system has failed."
All measures have already been implemented.
But there’s no national plan behind it, and we don’t acknowledge what doesn’t.”
Mutko’s words echoed the words of the head of Russia’s Independent Public Anti-Doping Commission, Vitali Smirnov.
Smirnov acknowledged past problems with the Russian Anti-Doping Agency in an interview with Russian outlet RSport earlier in the day, but added: “We’ve said on several occasions that the report contains controversial positions and regulations.”
There is no plan to accept the report unconditionally,” Smirnov said.
WADA suspended RUSADA’s right to perform doping inspections on Russian territory following the doping scandal in November 2015.
However, in June of this year, RUSADA was allowed to plan and coordinate testing under the supervision of international experts and UKAD this year.
WADA Director-General Olivia Negri, visiting the organization’s headquarters in Moscow in July, said RUSADA was “moving in the right direction” in an effort to restore its rights.
