To help you prevent the company from becoming the next Uber startup
Amy Erret used to want to investigate the happiness of employees at her e-commerce startup, but it didn’t work.
The responses received were often vague, unhelpful, and, worse, deceptive responses.
Even though she promises anonymity, some employees still don’t trust the process.
Madison Reed, a 75-member San Francisco-based e-commerce care company, said it “lacks consistency and objectivity in the responses.”
So she called for help.
A group of new human resources start-ups have sprung up, helping companies understand how employees feel.
They built and licensed software specifically designed to measure employee engagement, allowing companies to quickly conduct polls for specific teams and population groups, provide anonymous and complaint hotlines for employees, and, in some cases, allow whistleblowers to bypass executives and report directly to the board.
“Right now, you can use tools like Strava and Fitbit to track your health, but where’s the company’s Fitbit?” said Jim Barnett, co-founder and CEO of Redwood City startup Glint. Glint software analysis tools are used by various companies to measure employee engagement.
In three years, Érett said, she’s used Glint to gain more insights into people’s thoughts and feelings.
In addition to team-specific rapid surveys and polls, Glint offers company heat maps, which units have the most complaints, and which managers approve low scores at a glance — which allows her to get an in-depth understanding of the reasons.
Companies are beginning to realize that they must be proficient in controlling the company's workplace culture so as not to become the next Uber. Former Uber employees previously published a blog post describing the harassment environment, and these speakers were punished by the company, after Uber was caught up in a scandal.
For startups like Glint, the company’s desire for oversight is a lucrative business opportunity.
According to Let’s Talk Payments, a financial technology analysis website, the global governance, regulatory, and compliance industries could be worth more than $11.87 billion by 2020.
Convercent, a start-up in Denver, Colorado, is dedicated to helping companies prevent and detect bad behavior, and they saw an increase in interest and activity in this area during Uber’s reputation damage earlier this year.
Convercent has nearly 600 customers, including Airbnb, Microsoft and Tesla.
Uber recently contracted to become a customer.
Like Glint, Convercent allows companies to send customized “diagnosis” surveys, collecting confidential responses in real-time, and looking at thermal maps of their problem areas.
Convercent also provides an anonymous SMS hotline for employees to report bad behavior.
If the chairman is implicated, then the complaint will go directly to the board of directors.
Patrick Quinlan, founder and CEO of Convercent, said: “Public opinion courts have usurped the power of regulators.
If a company is found to treat employees badly or unethical, even if regulators don’t intervene, the company could face high consequences of consumer boycotts, loss of employees, and involvement in lawsuits, Quinland said.
Ruby Tuesday, a restaurant chain spanning more than 500 locations and with more than 25,000 employees, has been using Convercent for more than a year, ensuring that employees are aware of work guidelines and procedures, providing an easy way to connect with the company’s headquarters.
Until now, if an employee wants to report the problem, they must find the company headquarters phone number or e-mail, file a formal complaint, and hope that the headquarters can take this seriously.
It’s often a daunting and unsettling experience, says James Vitrano, Ruby Tuesday’s general counsel, and he doesn’t think there’s a good way to track employee complaints.
But now that the company uses Convercent, it’s able to more quickly identify and address issues that were previously unknown to executives in remote offices, cities, or states — such as discrimination, harassment, or unfair wage behavior.
“I’ll be able to get a closer, 360-degree view of the work experience,” said Vitrano, who is in charge of Ruby Tuesday’s risk management team.
“We are protecting our shareholders from employment lawsuits.”
Quinlan said that after the collapse of the Andorran accounting firm as a result of the violation of ethical norms by the Enron scandal, companies began to pay more attention to ethics, values and employee engagement in 2002.
But it wasn’t until “social media entered their own world” that companies realized they couldn’t stop their revelations from spreading virally online.
“Before the use of technology to monitor ethics, people saw hope as a strategy,” he said.
Glint and Convercent both provide their software as a service and charge companies recurring fees for product usage.
This business model and business opportunity is supported by venture capitalists who already support both startups.
In February, Convercent raised $10 million in funding from companies like Sapphire Ventures and Tola Capital, and its total capital rose to $47 million.
In November, Glint received $10 million in funding from Bessemer Venture Partners, with a total of $60 million.
Given the interconnectedness of companies, culture, and venture capital, these investments are hardly surprising.
A growing body of research shows that today’s employees have higher expectations for the workplace than ever before.
In a competitive market like Silicon Valley, high-paying water and interesting projects are just bets.
Employees want to feel that they are accepted and valued, and that their time is spent on a company with a positive mission.
“When people are happy to join the company, when their voices are heard and the work they’re doing is beneficial, they’re more committed to making the company successful,” said Nina McCartney, LinkedIn’s vice president of global benefits and employee experiences.
Investors pay off when their portfolio companies are doing well; companies perform well when employees are loyal and engaged.
It’s not surprising that third-party analytics tools promise to increase employee loyalty and engagement, they’ll be looking for financial support.
Workplace culture experts say it’s important to have employee engagement data.
However, this data is useless unless the company’s administrators take action.
In fact, if a company asks employees to provide feedback, it can expect that the company is undergoing changes.
If the change doesn’t progress fast enough, or if it doesn’t make any progress, it can lead to disappointment and leave the company.
Russell Ruth, head of consulting at business management firm Corte International, said: “If you’re looking for 4,000 pieces of advice, you’ll need to be ready for 4,000 conversations.” He’s seen some of the company’s over-reliances on data acquisition errors.
"Because if you don't follow up the whole time, the employee will think, 'Did you really hear what I said?
Do you care?
“If you don’t care, why should I care?”
Emerson said Madison Reed, which now conducts monthly employee surveys, has been able to take action on the day Glint reported the problem.
After collecting feedback from employees about their frustration with the speed and quality of their decisions, she was able to solve the problem by reorganizing her in-house team and increasing communication training.
Ruby Tuesday is gaining new insights into its employees, and in the long term they want to be able to make a shift in an industry known for its high brain drain to better retain employees.
“If you’re not committed to creating a culture of transparency, you’re going to lose talent,” Vitrano said.
“If you’ve lost talent, you’re going to lose customers.
And when you lose customers, you're going to lose company."
