The NCAA canceled twice-daily training, extending the Bears’ day-to-day training time.
It’s close to 11 p.m., and UCLA football team members linger next to the team’s new campus practice.
Some players have taken off their t-shirts, revealing their sweaty bodies after the first day of intensive training.
For a large part of the team, a few minutes is a break.
Players who take summer classes will return to their dormitories after three hours of practice to prepare for their final exams, and then return to the Wasserman Football Centre to attend meetings the next morning at 6:30.
“The next three days are a run-in for these guys,” Bear coach Jim Mora said as Monday was about to Tuesday.
The NCAA canceled twice-daily practice (aimed at reducing the burden on players), but actually lengthened their day-to-day training — at least until the end of Friday.
That’s why UCLA’s first three workouts are scheduled to start at 7:15 p.m. to accommodate a schedule where players are already crowded.
From the cancellation of training twice a day to the fact that it was done twice a day, the Bears began training almost a week before last season, and Mora trained at summer school for the first time in six seasons in Westwood.
They plan to start 18 training sessions on August 20th, and then start preparing for the official competition for the first Texas agricultural workers in September.
Mora said he supported the NCAA’s rule adjustment, although that would add another program to the player’s busy schedule.
“In three or four days, we’re going to give them a huge amount of training, it’s very challenging,” Mora said.
"They've just passed. They're honing, they're all great kids."
When I think about Saturday, we're going to have more time, it's really great."
At the end of the training camp, UCLA stops training. It's not an easy task, given the neighboring parking lot and the clear vision of the Luskin Center . The Luskin Center Superior Hotel has been slashed by a football thrown through the back of the doorpost protective net.
Starting this weekend, the Bears will be staying at the hotel. But Mora admitted that there could be problems once these rooms are vacant during the season.
“We’re going to take security measures,” Mora said.
"It's a real concern. The idea that someone might rent a room and sit in a room to record a training scene isn't far-fetched.
If you have a training tape, it’s also pretty good.
However, security measures will be taken there, and security measures will also be taken in parking lot No. 8.
We will use the existing resources to do the best.
I won’t worry too much about this.
We've made a plan."
