Facebook will launch a fact-checking function to combat fake news
Facebook announced on Thursday that it would send more potential hoax articles to third-party detection agencies and show the results below its original posts. Facebook, the world's largest online social network, is trying to crack down on so-called fake news.
Facebook said in a statement on its website that it will start using updated machines to learn to detect possible hoaxes and send them to fact-checkers, and may show fact-checking below the original post.
Facebook has been criticized as one of the main points of dissemination of so-called fake news, and many believe that Facebook influenced the 2016 U.S. presidential election.
This issue has also become a major political topic in Europe, before France's presidential election in May, voters encountered the proliferation of false stories, Germany was the first to pass the support for the fine plan before the September elections, if the social media network failed to remove hate posts in time, it will be fined.
Facebook said in a separate statement in Germany on Thursday that it was launching a new fact-checking feature test in the United States, France, the Netherlands and Germany.
Sarah Sue, product manager for the Facebook News feed, said: “In addition to seeing which stories have been challenged by third-party fact-checkers, people want to have more context to help them make informed decisions about what they read and share.”
She added that Facebook will continue to test its “relevant articles” feature and make other changes to its news feeds to reduce fake news.
The Story of an English Castle.
Most recently, the tiny Scottney Castle was featured in a condensed version of the music video and at one point became a refuge for Mrs. Margaret Thatcher during the seventies and eighties.
But Scottney has witnessed 700 years of turbulence in the comforts of the Belle River Gorge in Kent, and it is now the inspiration for a graphic novel written by Jamie Rhodes, a Yorkshire man who has a special interest in historical documents and has a tug head.
"I can see the handwriting of the ancients 300 years ago, and I found it too beautiful," he said.
“It’s what kind of relationships it’s to let this letter fall into my hands.”
Across the Middle Ages until the Edwardian era, the “An English Castle” book chronicles the families of Scottney, each of which was written by different future British artists such as Isaac Lenkiewicz, Briony May Smith, William Exley, Becky Palmer, and Isabel Greenberg.
This isn’t Rhodes’s first archival-based book: He also wrote The Teeth of the Dead in 2014, a book inspired by British library literature, a work he’s accomplished with uncontrollable creative passions.
He said, “I couldn’t suppress my impulse to write from the moment I saw the thumb imprint left on one side of the letter, written by an 18th-century double agent who worked for the Jacobites.”
“That’s his fingerprints!”
And the letters have always smelled of smoke, because at that time, you need to ignite to have light.
For us 300 years from now, the smell feels like they’re also around us.
“Oh, I like archival documents!”
For those impressed by the old paper, being able to get the support of the National Trust of England and the Arts Council and living in the castle for nearly four months (“To be honest, I’ve been here a bit longer”) seems fantastic and simply doesn’t believe it’s true.
Rhodes spent several days visiting Victoria’s “new” castles, flipping through archives, studying many treasures and antiquities of the estate, such as hyena heads hanging on the walls and bottles that had spent centuries in the moat.
He strolled over the land and ruins of the old castle, chatting with wanderers, gardeners, and sometimes artists illustrating his books.
Then into the evening, he would “drink some whiskey, and then write.”
Scottney’s cryptic history is not yet clear, because the National Trust only entered after the death of its last heir, Elizabeth Hessie, in 2006.
Ten years later, when Rodz arrived, the staff began to dispose of the files, describing them as "hundreds of years ago the nobles are leaving: "Oh, put it in the attic'."
Diaries, letters from the time of the British Empire, depict a map of each country’s territory: “You’d think land disputes would be boring,” Rhodes said, “but it happens that two families fight for a birch tree.”
“Leave now, guys.”
Some of the stories in the work take the castle as a starting point, leading to a broader study of British history, including The Labourer, which led to a man who left the new castle to participate in the 1381 Peasant Rebellion.
“The story in the book “The Knightly Families of Kent and Sussex” chronicles a riot that took place in the castle in 1380,” Rhodes said.
“Peasant insurrection began in that area, and I think it’s a good place to start an uprising.”
Maybe there’s a connection between these two?”
Other stories are deeply rooted in the castle’s history, such as “The Priest,” which tells the story of the Darrell family hiding a Jesuit pastor for up to seven years during the Reformation in Britain.
Or The Smuggler, the 18th-century smuggler Arthur Darrell, believed to hold a funeral for himself by filling his coffin with a stone (it was only a few years later that his coffin was exposed to the ground).
The Darrells is Rodz’s favorite Scottney Castle family: “These gentlemen seemed very scoundred, constantly borrowing money and buying things they couldn’t afford.
Generations of Darrell men solve their debt problems by marrying wealthy old women, and I think they are supposed to be a group of handsome lads who know how to pursue women.”
As his mission in the castle ended, Rhodes began to search for other bizarre writing experiences.
He spent two weeks in the Finnish wilderness completing a drawing novel about "El?¤m?¤nm?¤ki", an early 20th-century sanatorium that was slowly abandoned.
But Scottney had a special place for him.
“I’m sad to leave,” he said.
"It's like: 'Oh, I've got to deal with the real-world life.'"
