In recent years, Silicon Valley giants such as Google, Microsoft, Amazon and Facebook have engaged in an arms race in the field of artificial intelligence (AI).
Sex robots are on the rise, but we should be careful.
Just when you don’t think 2017 is going to be weirder, a new technology development report has been released in the field of sex robots.
Yes.
Nowadays, sex robots are a big deal.
Recently, the Responsible Robotics Foundation (FRR) released a report looking ahead to the development of sex robots over the next five to ten years.
According to Noel Shaki, an emeritus professor of robotics and artificial intelligence at the University of Sheffield and co-founder of FRR, we need to start taking the rise of sex robots seriously.
Customers can choose the robot’s eye color, nipple shape and nail type.
At the release of the new report, Shaki said: "People are laughing behind their backs, but the major companies do launch a lot of sex robots, and we're going to see more."
Modern sex dolls have evolved from inflatable dolls to robots, they have silicone skin, feel very warm, and deliberately use artificial intelligence to set up human-like sexual reactions.
Users can also “customize” their bots to suit their preferences, from the color of their eyes and nipples to the type or color of their nails.
But what’s even more disturbing is that a lot of robots with “personality” will make “replicating pampers” look advanced.
Modern sex toys have evolved into robots with silicone skin.
For example, the RealBotix robot can be defined according to the characteristics that the user finds attractive, such as shyness.
There’s also the Roxxxy Gold sex robot developed by True Companion, which can pre-set personality traits, including the slightly shy “cold-flavored Fara” and “Wild Wendy who is adventurous.”
“Roxxxy Gold bots have the personality that’s as appropriate to you as possible,” True Companion’s website relishes.
So she loves what you love, evil you.
She also had small emotions in those days like a normal person!
She can be lazy, she can be talkative, she can also be “sexually vigorous.”
The rise of sex robots has caused a lot of uneasiness – not just replacing real human relationships – but grovelling feminine traits as well as curvatory (recluded: resisting) rapism in Farah.
True Companion compares Roxxxy to a man’s vibrator on its website.
According to the site, “If a woman has a shock stick, then why can’t men have Roxxxy?”
However, these sex robots are not a simple fetish, nor are they just sex toys.
Their emergence and increasing maturation point to the darker, deeper parts of our culture: escaping from perfect gender equality to sex with surrender as an additional option.
Of course, most men don't have sex robots, and although FRR points out that sex robots are becoming more and more popular, they are still on the verge of consumer culture.
But what permeates from the edge allows us to see the course of our time.
A lot of people aren’t weird or aggressive until the free market gives them a chance.
It’s no coincidence that these fine sex robots have emerged in an era when women’s rights around the world are at stake, and there’s also a president in the White House boasting about sexual violence against women.
The most chilling part of the TV series "The Handmaid's Story" is not in the realistic picture, not in the hanging body, not in the pair of eyes that have been dug up, but in the excessive real imagination.
The Republic of Gilead was a jump, but it doesn’t seem far away right now.
The goal behind these sex bots is to do everything the technology possible to create the most real-world female body (albeit by the proportion of porn stars).
The creators of these robots wanted them to have human touch and imitate real human movements.
But bluntly speaking, their "personality" does not represent real women.
They can’t break up, they can’t leave.
They had no access to institutions and no follow-up stories, no independence at all, and no possibility of breaking the illusion of complete slavery.
In other words, they’re sex slaves.
Or according to the popular saying of 2017: maids.
