Trump's great achievements?
Mitt Rom Romney is the epitome of common sense.
Political differences have polarized American voters, and from a micro point of view, they have undermined America's family harmony. This is not surprising for the Trump era.
Around Christmas and around Thanksgiving, major U.S. newspapers were filled with the fear of how to vote for Trump’s relatives.
It’s often assumed that we’re doing everything we can to pierce our “filtration bubbles.”
What is less vocal is Trump’s power to bring his family together.
“I mention this because my cousin, a left-wing poet living in Chicago, who has been at odds with the Republicans in our family for a long time, is coming to New York this week to visit me.
She reminds me that uniting against a common enemy can have a powerful effect.
By working with Trump in the White House, everyone she knew, and even relatives of Republicans who had been squabbling for decades, were suddenly and strangely on the same front.
It's certainly a good thing if they're at the dinner table. But it's shocking to me that, from a broader perspective, it's going to cause significant risks.
Although the leftists have always been encouraged by Trump, the warping effect of his presidency has pushed us all to the right.
When we talk about “normalization” and Trump, we’re referring to the possibility of concern that his quirky behavior might one day stop.
However, there is also a more worrying long-term scenario where Trump wants to “normalize” relations with right-wing Republicans, who had previously opposed Mr. President’s standards and now suddenly looked like a symbol of reason.
I’ve found myself recently on Mitt Romney’s frenzied quirky nostalgia: putting dogs in cars, 14 percent income taxes (but at least we know his tax returns), for the crimes of these two actions do seem to be too minor in comparison to Trump.
When the Trump era is over, the next Republican candidate only needs to qualify for immeasurable improvements intellectually.
When my cousin lived in town, we took the kids to the carousel in New York Park, the biggest carousel operated by New York’s Central Park administration, and if you don’t need coffee, these three-dollar tickets are your most consumer value in the city.
The sound of the trojan is as loud as thunder, the central cylindrical is designed for fiberglass, all the clowns and animals twist their heads, looking at their shoulders in horror and sinister, while the jingling music is strangely ringing.
Too many vulgar cities are disappointing: the landscape overlooking high-rises has become old-fashioned, the skyline has become too familiar to provide too much excitement, and the city’s reality doesn’t sustain dreams.
Thirty-five years later, I haven’t completely forgotten that day, and I’ve found out that it wasn’t actually a Swiss hut, it’s an old Swiss Cottage located in a themed bar, “Ye Olde Swiss Cottage,” which I can’t remember, but near an intersection in north London.
However, Central Park’s carousel is still a magical experience, and for events where two 2-year-olds, an 11-year-old, and four girls in their 40s can attend, each having fun.
If I could evaluate the carousel, I’d give it a full score, but thankfully no one asked.
But this week at a department store, before swiping a credit card, I was asked to rate one to five stars on the “experience” of my transactions.
The same is true in the coffee shop on the corner of my street, in addition, you can choose to score with a 15% tip, which is to buy things on the counter.
The effect of this is not only to slow down the speed at which it is supposed to be a neutral transaction, but also to put pressure on the waiter, adding personal factors to unnecessary scenarios.
The poor woman in the department store smiled apologetically, and I was exhausted, and the credit card trading experience gave her five stars.
