China's household debt rate is closer to the United States than it can't afford to buy a house.
In recent times, everyone has been discussing the recent changes in real estate policy, whether the real estate policy will be loosened again, and the diversification of the main body of land policy supply, will the house prices decline?
However, when we were worried about real estate prices, worried about macroeconomic regulation, and worried about the future trend of the real estate market, the "People's Daily" once again wrote, the last time we specifically discussed the "People's Daily" comments on the tone of real estate control, and now the "People's Daily" wrote an article about a more serious topic than not being able to afford to buy a house.
Let’s talk today about the more serious problem than buying a house: paying off the loan.
China’s Growing Housing Debt Crisis
According to the data of the article, in November 2017, the National Financial and Development Laboratory of the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences and the National Balance Sheet Research Center released the "Report on China's Deleveraging Process in the third quarter", which pointed out that the leverage ratio of the residential sector continued to rise, rising from 47.4% in the second quarter of 2017 to 48.6% in the third quarter, up 1.3 percentage points, up 3.8 percentage points in the first three quarters of 2017.
The proportion of debt in the residential sector in the entire real economy has increased, and the leverage ratio has risen rapidly.
According to relevant studies, the debt of Chinese households in financial institutions is dominated by consumer loans, accounting for about two-thirds of residents' liabilities, which is about twice that of operating loans.
Among them, among consumer loans, medium- and long-term loans based on housing loans accounted for about 80%, but from an incremental point of view, short-term consumer loans grew faster than medium- and long-term loans.
In addition, China's household debt rate only counts the total amount of credit received by the family sector from financial institutions, while Chinese families also have many private loans, including borrowing money from relatives and friends, and there are large-scale hidden debts.
According to the statistics of the Suning Financial Research Institute, the leverage ratio of China's residents was only 3% in 1996 and only 18% in 2008, but since 2008, the leverage ratio of residents has begun to show a rapid growth trend, doubling to 36.4% in just six years.
In May last year, HSBC released a survey of nearly tens of thousands of respondents across nine countries.
The report is aimed at “millennial” young people in nine countries (born between 1980 and 2000).
According to the report, China's post-80s and post-90s young people's home ownership rate is as high as 70%, far ahead of second-place Mexico (46%), which is also the U.S. index (35%) twice the level.
This data tells us that there is a very likely problem, that is, compared to the inability to afford housing, China's residents at this stage of the largest potential "gray rhino" is expected to be unable to repay the debt.
Second, why is the housing debt crisis the most serious problem?
As we have analyzed before, although we have been saying that Chinese housing prices are higher, compared with other countries in the world, China's housing ownership rate is actually quite high, so instead of high housing prices can not afford housing, I am afraid to ask a lot of friends right now the question is that you still have a loan?
Looking at the world's real estate market, China's real estate market has started later, and there has not been a major real estate crisis except for Hainan, as we said before, but the origin of the 2008 global financial tsunami a decade ago was the real estate crisis in the United States.
The reasons for the U.S. real estate crisis are blamed on two houses: Fang Limei and Freedom.
At that time, in the golden age of the United States, the real estate company in order to expand the sale of the house with a very low down payment to the low-income people in the United States at that time, when the U.S. economy is good, the real estate market is also thriving, but not long, with the growth of the U.S. economy stagnated, resulting in a decline in the income of buyers, so a lot of household consumption expenditure began to decline.
However, at that time, the current situation in the United States was an integral part of U.S. household assets of financial assets and real estate assets, and almost all the household assets of low-income groups in the United States were real estate, while most of the assets in the top 20% of high-income groups were financial assets, resulting in the debt ratio of real estate in the United States.
It is a serious fact that these people are buying houses when the economy is better, so the money to pay the mortgage is also calculated by their better income, when there is a decline in income, the pressure of the mortgage is not only not reduced or even because some subprime plans have become a floating increase, so the real estate problems of these American families have gradually evolved into a real estate crisis.
As a result, some people can't afford mortgages because their income is too low, so the bank began to recover the property, and when the bank recovered the property from the buyers who owed the loan, they will sell it at a very low price, resulting in the decline in housing prices in the region, which led to a further decline in house prices, forming a vicious circle.
As a result, a huge vicious circle is formed:
“Sluggish economy – falling incomes – some people can’t afford mortgages – banks sail their homes at low prices – house prices are falling – more people stop repaying mortgages – banks are selling again – house prices are falling again...”
And the problem is more than that, because of the continuous decline in housing prices, we stop lending the house to the bank is not that you have no debt, if the bank auctioned the house to get the money you owed to the bank, then you still need to continue to pay back the money, simply assume that you used the down payment of 600,000, the loan 2.4 million to buy a house worth 3 million at that time, when the house fell to this house worth only 2 million, you no longer repay the house to the bank, after the bank auctioned the house.
As a result, in such a vicious circle one after another, the U.S. economy gradually collapsed, which eventually led to the US financial crisis in 2008.
Therefore, at this stage, China's household debt ratio has reached a high level of 46.8% is a very alarming problem, this data is still lower than the level of developed countries at present, but the beginning of the problem has begun to appear, the country from last year began to repeatedly emphasize the issue of deleveraging, this is a very prescient judgment, if the household debt ratio is allowed to continue to climb, it will inevitably lead to huge risks, so to prevent the emergence of financial risks.
As a result, after which the house is used to live, not for speculation positioning, the state to accelerate the establishment of multi-subject supply, multi-channel security, rental and purchase mergers and other measures to further resolve potential risks.
For our individual families, we must give up real estate speculative thinking, do their best when buying a house, to avoid their own family into a huge burden, only a reasonable assessment of their income and debt level is a healthy family wealth development path.
Author: Suning Financial Research Institute Special Researcher, financial columnist, financial commentator.
