The British inventor implanted a chip in his own body and waved to enter the house and enter the car.
A British inventor installs a chip for himself, just waving his hand to open the front door of his home, enter the office and even start the car, becoming the first person in the country.
Steven Northam, 33, implanted a small chip between his thumb and forefinger, and is now providing the service to companies and individuals who want to simplify their lives, a service enabled by his company BioTeq through technology.
He has teamed up with Dr. Jeff Watson, a senior adviser at Winchester Royal Hampshire Hospital in Hampshire, to ensure that the implantation process meets medical standards.
The technology is similar to a cat-and-dog chip and takes only 30 seconds.
Mr Northam’s company will also replace the locks of various rooms and vehicles to be compatible with the chip, which costs £300 for each lock.
The married father, who has a child, has now installed the technology for both his family and his BMW Z4, and he just puts his hands on the steering wheel to start the car.
