The White House cost thousands of dollars.
According to military records released by Time magazine, last month, the White House decided to transport a Marine helicopter to the south lawn to highlight the glory of U.S. manufacturing, costing up to $24,000.
Sikorsky VH-3D (also known as Marine Corps One when the president took the ride) is an important exhibit of the White House’s July 17 highlights U.S. construction projects.
President Donald Trump, Vice President Mike Pence, and senior White House officials toured manufactured products from all 50 states.
The exhibits include a Connecticut-built helicopter, a Maine yacht, a Wisconsin fire truck, and a Mississippi forklift.
According to the record of the First Marine Corps Helicopter (HMX-1), which is responsible for the operation of the helicopter, the White House Military Office asked the helicopter to show up by daily order (FRAGO), i.e. only 36 hours before the helicopter landed on the South Lawn to participate in the "special" event.
The day before the event, Commander HMX-1, in an e-mail sent to the Deputy Flight Commander of the superior Marine Corps, said, “I just want to confirm that you know, because this event is highly valued.”
This information indicates that the helicopter will arrive in the South Lawn at about 7 a.m. on July 17 to participate in 3 p.m. and leave after the lawn emptied from 6 p.m. to 9 p.m. the same day.
The information also said that in the event that the departure from the White House is delayed, the return flight will require the second group of crew members due to the duty time limit.
The original crew was on standby by the helicopter during their activities, while members of the security forces were on standby nearby when the aircraft was located on the ground.
The records obtained by Time magazine did not make it clear whether a second crew member would eventually be needed.
The Hachi Act prohibits federal employees from using government property for political purposes.
HMX-1 was asked whether the campaign violated the bill.
The commander wrote that “one of the questions we were asking was whether this was a violation of the Hachi Act and was told that there had been no violation”.
“It sounds like you’re locked up,” the deputy commander replied.
The White House defines this “Made in America” activity as an official activity and is therefore not governed by the Hachi Act.
However, even official events are politically charged.
At the event, the president promoted the then Senate issue: health care reform, and touting the control of government regulations.
It’s nothing new to use Marine One or Air Force One for political propaganda purposes.
Successive presidents have been required to travel on these planes.
However, it is not common to requisition it only for the purpose of taking photos.
In February, Trump received attention, when in Melbourne, Florida, the presidential plane slowly glided, and finally stopped at the background of his first campaign rally, and his Twitter account was full of complaints about the previous president's use of the presidential plane.
“The White House was thrilled by the ‘Made in America’ exhibition, which showcased products from all 50 states, made by U.S. workers using American materials,” White House deputy press secretary Lindsey Walters said.
The HMX-1 crew regularly conducts flight training near the Washington area and on the south lawn, practicing accurately placing the President of the United States in the White House.
During Trump’s G-20 summit in Germany in early July, HMX-1 practiced 58 landings at the White House.
One official said that the U.S.-manufacturing flight was included in this month’s crew training requirements.
The helicopter also flew an additional 11-hour "non-presidential support mission" on the same day.
The White House said taxpayers did not cover the cost of any other items at the event.
Sikorsky said the company was not involved in arranging a South Lawn helicopter display.
The HMX-1 does not keep a budget record for the presidential flight, so it is very difficult to determine the exact cost of the event.
However, according to the Department of Defense’s hourly expenses for the 2017 fiscal year, VH-3D’s Reimbursable Fees are $24,380 per flight hour.
Force records recorded that the aircraft flew for 30 minutes every time it flew from a base at Marine Corps Airport in Quantico, Virginia.
The White House said the cost of operating the helicopter was much lower than that.
Walters said: “It’s very misleading to estimate the taxpayer’s expenses based on reimbursable fees.
These estimated costs include personnel costs, maintenance costs and many other sunken costs included in the annual appropriations.
A 2015 RAND study showed that the Department of Defense’s reimbursable costs did not include personnel costs, but did include fuel costs, maintenance and contracting costs.
At that time, former White House press secretary Sean Spike defended the use of the helicopter for the event.
“The idea is this week to showcase what America makes.
And I know that Sikorsky and Connecticut are proud of it, and they have all contributed to our national security, and I’ve guessed there wouldn’t even be thousands of jobs that would depend on it.
And I think like most Americans, we’re all proud of this helicopter and other military equipment, and it’s the hard work of countless Americans.
So, of course, there’s not going to show that it’s untimely.”
It’s not clear whether Trump interacted with the helicopter when he toured the South Lawn at the event, but he did get in the driver’s seat of a fire truck.
Inside the White House, Trump addressed Sikorsky’s representatives, joking with the media about the company’s planes, which he owned.
He said, "I'm familiar with Sikorsky. I have three."
