U.N. says Afghanistan's democracy depends on women's political status
The head of the United Nations Assistance Mission in Afghanistan said Wednesday that elections involving only all members of society, including women, are representative.
The head of the United Nations Assistance Mission, Nicholas Heisom, said that hundreds of women are participating in the provincial council elections, three women are participating in vice presidential elections, and more than 30% of newly registered voters are women.
He said in a statement on Wednesday that “the status of women should not be underestimated.”
It is only when women are fully involved and included in the whole stage of the electoral process that there are truly representative and credible elections.
Afghanistan's provincial parliamentary and presidential elections took place on April 5.
The Taliban intimidated an electoral process that coincided with the transition from international military operations to the status of advisers.
This year’s elections are the first peaceful transfer of power from one democratically elected to the next.
Heysom said that women's importance will promote democracy in Afghanistan.
According to a report released by the Independent Electoral Commission, 1.2 million out of 3.6 million registered voters are women.
