GMB focuses on investments at McVitie’s Glasgow plant
The union wants the management of biscuit maker McVeta to guarantee a long-term commitment to production at the Rasgo plant.
Britain's trade union Scotland said it had remained silent about the future of investment in the Tollcross plant.
With more than 500 employees, the plant manufactures products such as Hobnobs and Rich tea.
Pladis, the owner of the company, said that over the past few years, the company has invested more than £5 million in the plant.
He added that if there is a change in factory operations, employees and union representatives “will be the first to know.”
In a letter to the company’s director, the Scottish chapter of the General Union of the United Kingdom called on the company to “resolve the problem of the unscheduled transfer of funds to the modernization of the production line”.
Drew Duffy, senior sponsor of the GTT, said: "We are not able to get a clear picture of Tollcross's future business needs and investment plans from the plant's general manager, which is of deep concern to our members.
Generations of families are working here, and the high concentration of the local community and Greater Glasgow’s workforce is an important pillar of high unemployment and social deprivation for decent employment in areas.
Therefore, from the perspective of employment, compensation and economic prosperity, in the context of Brexit, the rising cost of living and the long-term decline in manufacturing, there is an urgent need in the eastern part of Glasgow to continuously expand the production network.”
A spokesman for Pladis said: “The director of Tollcross just received a letter yesterday from the Scottish Section of the General Union of England, and the company will respond immediately because of our emphasis on ongoing engagement with our employees and trade union representatives.
Two months ago, we held a regular meeting in Tollcross with our employees and representatives of the British Federation of Trade Unions to discuss a number of issues publicly.
“We had discussions with our employees, and then, like all other companies operating in highly competitive and economically uncertain environments, we reviewed the ongoing operating models that we applied to businesses and factories in order to remain competitive.”
She added: “In recent years, we have invested more than £5 million in our Tollcross plant, especially in terms of production lines and operational improvements.
We are fully aware of Tollcross's historical position as a production site, and if there is any change in our operations at the plant, employees and union representatives will be the first to know."
Xinhua News Agency, Tibet, May 11 (Reporter Chen Taobin) Nima Tsering, deputy director of the Sports Bureau of the Tibet Autonomous Region, said on the 10th that in the future, the clean-up of domestic garbage on the northern slope of Mount Everest will move from the public welfare actions of the past to the normalized behavior led by the Chinese government and with the participation of the international community.
On May 10, environmental volunteers loaded classified garbage into trucks.
In the past ten years, with the sharp increase in the number of Everest climbers and tourists, the environmental protection problems of the mountain location have become increasingly prominent.
In the spring of 1997, the Tibet Autonomous Region Mountaineering Association launched its first cleaning campaign on the northern slope of Mount Everest, cleaning up a number of oxygen cylinders left over from the 1960s and 1970s. The video was also specially broadcast at the World Climate Conference in Tokyo that year, attracting the world's attention.
According to Nima Tsering, after 1997, the public welfare team successively cleaned the northern slope of Mount Everest, but most of the participants were non-professionals.
